\vspace{3 mm}
In these notes, the notion of the determinant of a linear transformation will be developed and motivated accordingly. To achieve this without loss of generality, we consider an $n$-dimensional vector space $\bm V$ over the field $\mathbb R$, which (optionally) admits an inner product $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$, and an orthonormal basis given by $\beta=\left\{\bm e_i\right\}_{i=1}^n$.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
We now aim to construct a (hyper) volume-form which accepts $n$ vectors from $\bm V$ that span an $n$-dimensional oriented parallelepiped, and then outputs the respective signed (hyper) volume. In particular, consider the multi-linear form $\omega:\bm V^n\to\mathbb R$ (i.e. $\omega\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V^n,\mathbb{R})$) which satisfies the following properties:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The signed (hyper) volume of the $n$-dimensional (hyper) cube spanned by the (positively oriented) orthonormal basis $\beta$ is normalized to unity:
    $$\omega\left(\bm e_1,\dots,\bm e_n\right)=1$$
    \item The signed (hyper) volume of any degenerate $n$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by any redundant collection of $n$ vectors is necessarily null:
    $$\omega\left(\dots,v,\dots,v,\dots\right)=0\quad\text{for any}\quad v\in\bm V$$
\end{itemize}
Using the degenerate (hyper) volume property in conjunction with multi-linearity, the action of $\omega$ on any linearly dependent input can be evaluated. Specifically, consider linearly dependent vectors $w_1,\dots,w_n\in\bm V$, and without loss of generality, assume $w_n\in\mathrm{span}\left(w_1,\dots,w_{n-1}\right)$, implying $\exists\,a_1,\dots,a_n\in\mathbb{R}$ such that $w_n=a_1w_1+\cdots+a_{n-1}w_{n-1}$, from which it entails:
$$\omega\left(w_1,\dots,w_{n-1},w_n\right)=\omega\left(w_1,\dots,w_{n-1},\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}a_iw_i\right)=\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}a_i\,\omega\underbrace{\left(w_1,\dots,w_{n-1},w_i\right)}_{\text{redundant}}=\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}a_i\cdot0=0$$
Likewise, observe how these properties imply anti-symmetry/alternation. To be specific, given any $u,v\in\bm V$:
\begin{align*}
&\omega\left(\dots,u+v,\dots,u+v,\dots\right)=\omega\left(\dots,u,\dots,u+v,\dots\right)+\omega\left(\dots,v,\dots,u+v,\dots\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=\underbrace{\omega\left(\dots,u,\dots,u,\dots\right)}_{=0}+\omega\left(\dots,u,\dots,v,\dots\right)+\omega\left(\dots,v,\dots,u,\dots\right)+\underbrace{\omega\left(\dots,v,\dots,v,\dots\right)}_{=0}
\\[2 mm]
&=\omega\left(\dots,u,\dots,v,\dots\right)+\omega\left(\dots,v,\dots,u,\dots\right)=0
\quad\implies\quad\omega\left(\dots,v,\dots,u,\dots\right)=-\omega\left(\dots,u,\dots,v,\dots\right)
\end{align*}
Now, let $S_n=\left\{\sigma:\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}\to\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}|\,\sigma\;\text{is bijective}\right\}$ be the set of all possible permutations/orderings of $n$ elements. We call $\tau\in S_n$ a transposition if $\exists\,i,j$ such that $\tau(i)=j$, $\tau(j)=i$, and $\tau(k)=k$ for any $k\neq i,j$. Given any $\sigma\in S_n$, there exists a collection of transpositions $\tau_1,\dots,\tau_{N(\sigma)}\in S_n$, for some $N(\sigma)\leq\frac{1}{2}n(n-1)$ such that $\sigma=\tau_1\circ\cdots\circ\tau_{N(\sigma)}$. We now define the permuted volume-form as such:
$$\omega_\sigma\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)\coloneq\omega\left(v_{\sigma(1)},\dots,v_{\sigma(n)}\right)\quad\text{for any}\quad v_1,\dots,v_n\in\bm V\quad\text{and}\quad\sigma\in S_n$$
With this, via the transposition decomposition $\sigma=\tau_1\circ\cdots\circ\tau_{N(\sigma)}\in S_n$, we can always write the following:
\begin{align*}
\omega_{\sigma}\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)&=\omega_{\tau_1\circ\cdots\circ\tau_{N(\sigma)}}\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)=\omega_{\tau_1\circ\cdots\circ\tau_{N(\sigma)-1}}\left(v_{\tau_{N(\sigma)}(1)},\dots,v_{\tau_{N(\sigma)}(n)}\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=-\omega_{\tau_1\circ\cdots\circ\tau_{N(\sigma)-1}}\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)
\\[1 mm]
\text{(induction)}\quad&\;\;\vdots
\\[1 mm]
&=\left(-1\right)^{N(\sigma)}\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)\quad\implies\quad\omega_\sigma=(-1)^{N(\sigma)}\omega
\end{align*}
\newpage
In order to validate the uniqueness of $\omega_{\sigma}$, and thus $(-1)^{N(\sigma)}$, suppose $\omega_{\sigma}=a\omega$ and $\omega_{\sigma}=b\omega$ for some $a,b=\pm1$. We aim to show uniqueness using the volumetric normalization/orientation property: 
$$a\omega=b\omega\quad\implies\quad\left(a-b\right)\omega=0\quad\implies\quad\left(a-b\right)\underbrace{\omega\left(\bm e_1,\dots,\bm e_n\right)}_{=1}=0\quad\implies\quad a=b$$
It thereby follows that $\omega_{\sigma}$ is uniquely determined by $\sigma$, and so the sign of the permutation $\sigma$, which will be defined by $\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)\coloneq(-1)^{N(\sigma)}$, is well defined. We therefore arrive at the following identity:
$$\omega\left(v_{\sigma(1)},\dots,v_{\sigma(n)}\right)=\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)\,\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)\quad\text{for any}\quad v_1,\dots,v_n\in\bm V$$
Since $\sigma\in S_n$ is a bijection, it necessarily has an inverse $\sigma^{-1}\in S_n$. Moreover, because these two permutations compose to $\sigma\circ\sigma^{-1}=\mathrm{id}_{\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}}\in S_n$, one has that:
$$\omega=\omega_{\mathrm{id}_{\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}}}=\omega_{\sigma\circ\sigma^{-1}}=\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)\omega_{\sigma^{-1}}=\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma^{-1}\right)\omega\quad\implies\quad\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma^{-1}\right)=\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)$$
We now desire an algorithm for the function $\mathrm{sgn}:S_n\to\pm1$. With some foresight at hand, consider the auxiliary candidate as follows:
$$P:\sigma\in S_n\mapsto\prod_{1\leq i<j\leq n}\left(\sigma(j)-\sigma(i)\right)$$
This function (i.e. $P$) has two characteristic properties that make its relationship to $\mathrm{sgn}:S_n\to\pm1$ concrete. In particular, observe both the magnitude and the sign of $P(\sigma)$ as below:
$$|P(\sigma)|=\prod_{1\leq i<j\leq n}\left|\sigma(j)-\sigma(i)\right|=\prod_{1\leq i<j\leq n}\left|j-i\right|=\prod_{1\leq i<j\leq n}\left(j-i\right)$$
\begin{center}
\vspace{3 mm}
    ------ and ------
\vspace{3 mm}
\end{center}
$$\mathrm{sgn}\left(P(\sigma)\right)=\prod_{1\leq i<j\leq n}\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma(j)-\sigma(i)\right)=\prod_{i=1}^{N(\sigma)}\underbrace{\mathrm{sgn}\left(\tau_i\right)}_{=-1}\prod_{j=1}^{n-N(\sigma)}\underbrace{\mathrm{sgn}\left(\mathrm{id}_{\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}}\right)}_{=1}=(-1)^{N(\sigma)}=\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)$$
\begin{center}
\vspace{3 mm}
    ------ which implies ------
\vspace{3 mm}
\end{center}
$$P(\sigma)=\mathrm{sgn}\left(P(\sigma)\right)|P(\sigma)|=\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)\prod_{1\leq i<j\leq n}\left(j-i\right)=\prod_{1\leq i<j\leq n}\left(\sigma(j)-\sigma(i)\right)$$
\vspace{-1.5 mm}\\
Upon a simple rearrangement of the above relationships, we arrive at a precise (conventional) algorithm for computing the sign of any permutation. The algorithm is given below: 
$$\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)=\prod_{1\leq i<j\leq n}\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma(j)-\sigma(i)\right)=\prod_{1\leq i<j\leq n}\frac{\sigma(j)-\sigma(i)}{j-i}\quad\text{for any}\quad\sigma\in S_n$$
Observe that if $v_{i_1},\dots,v_{i_n}\in\bm V$ is indexed redundantly, then $\omega\left(v_{i_1},\dots,v_{i_n}\right)=0$. Otherwise, there exists a permutation $\sigma\in S_n$ such that $\sigma(k)=i_k$, and thus $\omega\left(v_{i_1},\dots,v_{i_n}\right)=\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)$. This casework breakdown is encapsulated by the Levi-Civita symbol $\varepsilon$ as follows:
\[
\varepsilon_{i_1\dots i_n}=
\begin{cases}
\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right),\quad&\exists\,\sigma\in S_n:\sigma(k)=i_k
\\
0,&\exists\,j,k:i_j=i_k
\end{cases}
\]
It thereby follows that the signed (hyper) volume generated by the (potentially redundant) collection of vectors $v_{i_1},\dots,v_{i_n}\in\bm V$ with index structure $\left\{i_1,\dots,i_n\right\}\subseteq\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}$ can be expressed as follows:
$$\omega\left(v_{i_1},\dots,v_{i_n}\right)=\varepsilon_{i_1\dots i_n}\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)$$
At this point, we will study how the volume-form interacts with a composite linear transformation. That is, under the linear map $T:\bm V\to\bm V$ (i.e. $T\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$), the restriction $\left.\omega\right|_{T(\bm V)^n}$ will be analyzed in order to understand how $T$ distorts signed (hyper) volumes.
\newpage
To be more precise, for any such $T\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$, we define the pullback with respect to the linear map $T$, hereby denoted by $T^*:\mathscr{L}(\bm V^n,\mathbb{R})\to\mathscr{L}(\bm V^n,\mathbb{R})$, as such:
$$T^*:\theta\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V^n,\mathbb{R})\mapsto\left(T^*\theta:v_1,\dots,v_n\in\bm V\mapsto\theta\left(T(v_1),\dots,T(v_n)\right)\right)$$
Note for later reference that $T^*$ is a linear operator in it of itself. Specifically, given any vectors $v_1,\dots,v_n\in\bm V$, for any $\lambda\in\mathbb{R}$, and for any $\theta,\phi\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V^n,\mathbb{R})$, one has:
\begin{align*}
    T^*(\theta+\lambda\phi)\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)&=(\theta+\lambda\phi)\left(T(v_1),\dots,T(v_n)\right)=\theta\left(T(v_1),\dots,T(v_n)\right)+\lambda\phi\left(T(v_1),\dots,T(v_n)\right)
    \\[4 mm]
    &=T^*\theta\left(v_1,\dots,v_n \right)+\lambda T^*\phi\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)=\left(T^*\theta+\lambda T^*\phi\right)\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)
    \\[4 mm]
    \text{(holds $\forall\,v_1,\dots,v_n$)}\quad&\implies T^*(\theta+\lambda\phi)=T^*\theta+\lambda T^*\phi\quad\implies\quad T^*\in\mathscr{L}(\mathscr{L}(\bm V^n,\mathbb{R}))
\end{align*}
Now, we construct the pulled-back volume-form given by $T^*\omega\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V^n,\mathbb{R})$ accordingly. Also, keep in mind that $\exists!\,[T]_\beta\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ such that $T(\bm e_j)=\sum_{i=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{ij}\bm e_i$. The following computation directly follows:
\begin{align*}
&T^*\omega\left(\bm e_1,\dots,\bm e_n\right)=\omega\left(T(\bm e_1),\dots,T(\bm e_n)\right)=\omega\left(\,\sum_{i_1=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{i_11}\bm e_{i_1},\dots,\sum_{i_n=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{i_nn}\bm e_{i_n}\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=\sum_{i_1=1}^n\cdots\sum_{i_n=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{i_11}\cdots\left([T]_\beta\right)_{i_nn}\omega\left(\bm e_{i_1},\dots,\bm e_{i_n}\right)=\sum_{1\leq i_1,\dots,i_n\leq n} \left[\,\prod_{j=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{i_jj}\right]\omega\left(\bm e_{i_1},\dots,\bm e_{i_n}\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=\sum_{1\leq i_1,\dots,i_n\leq n} \left[\,\prod_{j=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{i_jj}\right]\varepsilon_{i_1\dots i_n}\underbrace{\omega\left(\bm e_1,\dots,\bm e_n\right)}_{=1}=\sum_{1\leq i_1,\dots,i_n\leq n}\varepsilon_{i_1\dots i_n}\prod_{j=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{i_jj}
\end{align*}
In fact, if we define the determinant $\det:\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}\to\mathbb{R}$ as the function alluded to above (given below), then this computation could be made illustratively simpler:
$$
\det(A)\coloneq\sum_{1\leq i_1,\dots,i_n\leq n} \varepsilon_{i_1\dots i_n}\prod_{j=1}^nA_{i_jj}
\quad\implies\quad
T^*\omega\left(\bm e_1,\dots,\bm e_n\right)=\det\left([T]_\beta\right)
$$
Alternatively, this identity can be reformatted via a restriction to the basis of the pulled-back volume-form:
$$T^*\omega\left(\bm e_1,\dots,\bm e_n\right)=\det\left([T]_\beta\right)\omega\left(\bm e_1,\dots,\bm e_n\right)$$
Overall, this perspective makes the meaning of the determinant clear. For any $A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$, by insisting upon the interpretation $A_{*j}=[T(\bm e_j)]_\beta$ for some linear map $T\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$, the scalar $\det(A)$ naturally computes the signed (hyper) volume of the deformed parallelepiped spanned by $\left\{\bm e_i\right\}_{i=1}^n$ under the map $T$. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Since all of the redundant combinations of $i_1,\dots,i_n\in\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}$ correspond to $\varepsilon=0$, within the above definition of the determinant, summing over all non-redundant $1\leq i_1,\dots,i_n\leq n$ is equivalent to summing over all permutations $\sigma\in S_n$ such that $\sigma(j)=i_j$. We can thus deduce the Leibniz determinant formula:
$$\det\left(A\right)=\sum_{\sigma\in S_n}\varepsilon_{\sigma(1)\dots\sigma(n)}\prod_{j=1}^nA_{\sigma(j)j}=\sum_{\sigma\in S_n}\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)\prod_{j=1}^nA_{\sigma(j)j}$$
One simple result from this formulation is that the determinant is invariant under matrix transposition. In order to streamline the analysis, take note that $\prod_{j=1}^nA_{\sigma(j)j}=\prod_{j=1}^nA_{j\sigma^{-1}(j)}$. We can therefore write:
\begin{align*}
    \det\left(A^\top\right)&=\sum_{\sigma\in S_n}\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)\prod_{j=1}^n\left(A^\top\right)_{\sigma(j)j}=\sum_{\sigma\in S_n}\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)\prod_{j=1}^nA_{j\sigma(j)}=\sum_{\sigma\in S_n}\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)\prod_{j=1}^nA_{\sigma^{-1}(j)j}
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\sum_{\sigma\in S_n}\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma^{-1}\right)\prod_{j=1}^nA_{\sigma(j)j}=\sum_{\sigma\in S_n}\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)\prod_{j=1}^nA_{\sigma(j)j}=\det\left(A\right)
\end{align*}
\newpage
Given a secondary linear transformation $U\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$ with the property that $u_i=U(\bm e_i)$ for convenience, consider the signed (hyper) volume generated by $u_1,\dots,u_n$ as such:
$$\omega\left(u_1,\dots,u_n\right)=\omega\left(U(\bm e_1),\dots,U(\bm e_n)\right)=U^*\omega\left(\bm e_1,\dots,\bm e_n\right)=\det\left([U]_\beta\right)=\det\left([u_1]_\beta\cdots[u_n]_\beta\right)$$
Some immediate consequences from this perspective follow directly for the determinant due to the inherent properties of the volume-form $\omega$ as demonstrated (contextually) below:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The determinant of the identity matrix is unity:
    $$\det\left(I\right)=\det\left([\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}]_\beta\right)=\det\left([\bm e_1]_\beta\cdots[\bm e_n]_\beta\right)=\omega\left(\bm e_1,\dots,\bm e_n\right)=1$$
    \item The determinant is a linear (in)dependence detector:
    $$\det\left([u_1]_\beta\cdots[u_n]_\beta\right)=\omega\left(u_1,\dots,u_n\right)=0\quad\iff\quad u_1,\dots,u_n\quad\text{are linearly dependent}$$
    \item The determinant is column-wise linear:
    \begin{align*}
        \det\left([u_1]_\beta\cdots[v+\lambda w]_\beta\cdots[u_n]_\beta\right)&=\omega\left(u_1,\dots,v+\lambda w,\dots,u_n\right)
        \\[4 mm]
        &=\omega\left(u_1,\dots,v,\dots,u_n\right)+\lambda\omega\left(u_1,\dots,w,\dots,u_n\right)
        \\[4 mm]
        &=\det\left([u_1]_\beta\cdots[v]_\beta\cdots[u_n]_\beta\right)+\lambda\det\left([u_1]_\beta\cdots[w]_\beta\cdots[u_n]_\beta\right)
    \end{align*}
    \item The determinant is column-wise alternating:
    $$\det\left(\cdots[u]_\beta\cdots[v]_\beta\cdots\right)=\omega\left(\dots,u,\dots,v,\dots\right)=-\omega\left(\dots,v,\dots,u,\dots\right)=-\det\left(\cdots[v]_\beta\cdots[u]_\beta\cdots\right)$$
\end{itemize}
An important observation to make is that since $\det\left(A^T\right)=\det\left(A\right)$, the above properties, which all apply to the column spaces of the respective determinant matrix arguments, also apply to the respective row spaces. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
In particular, with the understanding that $u_1,\dots,u_n$ is linearly dependent if and only if the collection of columns $[u_1]_\beta,\dots,[u_n]_\beta$ is linearly dependent, which is to say $\dim\left(\mathrm{Col}\left([u_1]_\beta\cdots[u_n]_\beta\right)\right)<n$, that premise of linear dependence is equivalent to $\dim\left(\mathrm{Row}\left([u_1]_\beta\cdots[u_n]_\beta\right)\right)<n$ as well. As a general summary:
$$
A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}\quad\text{is invertible}\quad\iff\quad\mathrm{rank}\left(A\right)=\mathrm{rank}\left(A^\top\right)=n\quad\iff\quad\det\left(A\right)\neq0
$$
In other words, if the columns of a matrix are linearly dependent, then so are its rows (and vice versa). If either the rows or columns are linearly dependent, then the matrix has a null determinant (and vice versa). Otherwise, the determinant of the matrix is non-zero. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
It likewise follows that the determinant is row-wise linear. In particular, upon transposing the determinant matrix arguments of the linearity property: $$\det\left(\left([u_1]_\beta\cdots[v+\lambda w]_\beta\cdots[u_n]_\beta\right)^\top\right)=\det\left(\left([u_1]_\beta\cdots[v]_\beta\cdots[u_n]_\beta\right)^\top\right)+\lambda\det\left(\left([u_1]_\beta\cdots[w]_\beta\cdots[u_n]_\beta\right)^\top\right)$$
\begin{center}
\vspace{3 mm}
    ------------ or equivalently ------------
\vspace{3 mm}
\end{center}
$$
\det
\begin{pmatrix}
\left([u_1]_\beta\right)^\top\\
\vdots\\
\left([v+\lambda w]_\beta\right)^\top\\
\vdots\\
\left([u_n]_\beta\right)^\top
\end{pmatrix}
=\det\begin{pmatrix}
\left([u_1]_\beta\right)^\top\\
\vdots\\
\left([v]_\beta\right)^\top\\
\vdots\\
\left([u_n]_\beta\right)^\top
\end{pmatrix}
+\lambda\det\begin{pmatrix}
\left([u_1]_\beta\right)^\top\\
\vdots\\
\left([w]_\beta\right)^\top\\
\vdots\\
\left([u_n]_\beta\right)^\top
\end{pmatrix}
$$
\vspace{1.5 mm}\\
By inspection, adding a scalar multiple of any row (or column respectively) to any other row (or column respectively) within a matrix does not change its determinant. Contextually (above), if $w\in\left\{u_1,\dots,u_n\right\}$, then $\det\left([u_1]_\beta\cdots[w]_\beta\cdots[u_n]_\beta\right)=0$ because the vectors $u_1,\dots,v,\dots,u_n$ are linearly dependent.
\newpage
Analogously, due to invariance with respect to matrix transposition, the determinant is row-wise alternating. In particular, upon transposing the determinant matrix arguments of the alternation property:
$$\det\left(\left(\cdots[u]_\beta\cdots[v]_\beta\cdots\right)^\top\right)=-\det\left(\left(\cdots[v]_\beta\cdots[u]_\beta\cdots\right)^\top\right)$$
\begin{center}
\vspace{3 mm}
    ------------ or equivalently ------------
\vspace{3 mm}
\end{center}
$$
\det
\begin{pmatrix}
    \vdots\\
    \left([u]_\beta\right)^\top\\
    \vdots\\
    \left([v]_\beta\right)^\top\\
    \vdots
\end{pmatrix}
=-\det
\begin{pmatrix}
    \vdots\\
    \left([v]_\beta\right)^\top\\
    \vdots\\
    \left([u]_\beta\right)^\top\\
    \vdots
\end{pmatrix}
$$
\vspace{1.5 mm}\\
Due to the fact that internal row operations will scale the resultant determinant non-degenerately, under the mapping $A\mapsto\mathrm{RREF}(A)$, it thereby follows that $\det\left(A\right)=\pm\det\left(\mathrm{RREF}(A)\right)$, and so the matrix $A$ is invertible (i.e. $\det\left(A\right)\neq0$) if and only if $\mathrm{RREF}(A)$ is invertible (i.e. $\det\left(\mathrm{RREF}(A)\right)\neq0$).
\vspace{3 mm}\\
As a quick interlude, since $\omega$ is multi-linear, it contextually follows that $\omega\left(\lambda v_1,\dots,\lambda v_n\right)=\lambda^n\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)$, and thus for any $A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$, $\det\left(\lambda A\right)=\lambda^n\det\left(A\right)$ due to the previous reasoning (column-wise linearity). 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Now, upon synthesizing the fact that the pullbacks $T^*,U^*\in\mathscr{L}(\mathscr{L}(\bm V^n,\mathbb{R}))$ are linear operators with how the pulled-back volume-forms $T^*\omega,U^*\omega\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V^n,\mathbb{R})$ necessarily act on the basis $\beta$, the intrinsic nature of the determinant can be uncovered as such:
\begin{align*}
    T^*\omega\left(u_1,\dots,u_n\right)&=T^*\left(U^*\omega\right)\left(\bm e_1,\dots,\bm e_n\right)=T^*\left(\det\left([U]_\beta\right)\omega\right)\left(\bm e_1,\dots,\bm e_n\right)=\det\left([U]_\beta\right)T^*\omega\left(\bm e_1,\dots,\bm e_n\right)
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\det\left([U]_\beta\right)\det\left([T]_\beta\right)=\det\left([T]_\beta\right)\det\left([U]_\beta\right)=\det\left([T]_\beta\right)\omega\left(u_1,\dots,u_n\right)
\end{align*}
In particular, because there always exists a linear mapping $U:\bm e_i\mapsto u_i$ for any such vectors $u_1,\dots, u_n$, the above identity can be represented as an equivalence of maps:
$$T^*\omega=\det\left([T]_\beta\right)\omega$$
Upon closer inspection of the above reasoning, since matrix multiplication is defined in order to encode map composition accordingly, for any $T,U\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$, the following composition/multiplication law for the determinant emerges instantly:
$$\det\left([T]_\beta[U]_\beta\right)=\det\left([T\circ U]_\beta\right)=(T\circ U)^*\omega\left(\bm e_1,\dots,\bm e_n\right)=T^*U^*\omega\left(\bm e_1\dots,\bm e_n\right)=\det\left([T]_\beta\right)\det\left([U]_\beta\right)$$
Since the maps $T,U\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$ are arbitrary, they can be chosen such that, for any $A,B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$, one has that $[T]_\beta=A$ and $[U]_\beta=B$. From such a vantage point, the above property can be re-casted as below:  
$$\det\left(AB\right)=\det\left(A\right)\det\left(B\right)\quad\text{for any}\quad A,B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$$
An immediate consequence of this multiplication property is a direct means of computing the determinant of a matrix inverse. Specifically, assuming that $\det\left(A\right)\neq0$ (i.e. $A$ is invertible), one has:
$$\det\left(A^{-1}\right)\det\left(A\right)=\det\left(A^{-1}A\right)=\det\left(I\right)=1\quad\implies\quad\det\left(A^{-1}\right)=\det\left(A\right)^{-1}$$ 
Upon combining both of these properties, it can be deduced that similar matrices in fact have the same determinant. That is, given $A,B,Q\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$, provided that $A=QBQ^{-1}$ (i.e. $A\sim B$), it then follows:
$$\det\left(A\right)=\det\left(QBQ^{-1}\right)=\det\left(Q\right)\det\left(B\right)\det\left(Q^{-1}\right)=\det\left(Q\right)\det\left(B\right)\det\left(Q\right)^{-1}=\det\left(B\right)$$
\newpage
As a critical insight, for any other basis $\gamma$ of $\bm V$, because $[T]_\beta\sim[T]_\gamma$ per the change of basis matrix given by $[\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}]_\gamma^\beta$, and thus  $[T]_\beta=[\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}]_\gamma^\beta\,[T]_\gamma\,[\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}]_\beta^\gamma$, it immediately follows that the determinant is basis independent as depicted accordingly:
$$\det\left([T]_\beta\right)=\det\left([T]_\gamma\right)\quad\text{for any basis $\gamma$ of $\bm V$}$$
Consequently, the determinant is an intrinsic quantity associated with an underlying linear map, and so the pulled-back volume-form (map) identity can be re-casted intrinsically as such:
$$T^*\omega=\det\left(T\right)\omega\quad\text{such that}\quad\det\left(T\right)\coloneq\det\left([T]_\gamma\right)\quad\text{for any basis $\gamma$ of $\bm V$}$$
From a distinct perspective, because the pullback $T^*\in\mathscr{L}(\mathscr{L}(\bm V^n,\mathbb{R}))$ can be considered a linear operator in it of itself, the above identity can be viewed as an eigen equation, where $\omega\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V^n,\mathbb{R})$ is an eigenvector with a corresponding eigenvalue $\det\left(T\right)\in\mathbb{R}$ of the linear operator $T^*$. This perspective alone could have been used to show that $\det\left([T]_\beta\right)$, an eigenvalue, is intrinsic to the linear map $T$ and not the basis $\beta$.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Overall, with this new viewpoint, the determinant can be interpreted as the signed (hyper) volumetric scale factor from any non-degenerate parallelepiped to its image under the linear map. In particular, the determinant of the $n$-dimensional linear map $T$ can be characterized as below:
$$\det\left(T\right)=\frac{T^*\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)}{\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)}\quad\text{for any linearly independent}\quad v_1,\dots,v_n\in\bm V$$
Though trivial, we can directly deduce the determinant of the identity map to be unity as shown below:
$$\det\left(\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}\right)=\det\left([\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}]_\beta\right)=\det\left(I\right)=1$$
Likewise, the multiplication/composition property of determinants can be re-casted intrinsically as such:
$$\det\left(T\circ U\right)=\det\left([T\circ U]_\beta\right)=\det\left([T]_\beta\right)\det\left([U]_\beta\right)=\det\left(T\right)\det\left(U\right)$$
From the same perspective, recalling that $\bm V$ is a vector space over $\mathbb{R}$ (i.e. not $\mathbb{C}$), the transpose property of the determinant can be re-casted intrinsically using the adjoint $\dagger$ as such:
$$\det\left(T^\dagger\right)=\det\left(\left[T^\dagger\right]_\beta\right)=\det\left(\left([T]_\beta\right)^\top\right)=\det\left([T]_\beta\right)=\det\left(T\right)$$
Additionally, if the linear transformation $T$ is invertible, then the corresponding matrix inversion determinant property can be re-casted intrinsically as such:  
$$\det\left(T^{-1}\right)=\det\left(\left[T^{-1}\right]_\beta\right)=\det\left(\left([T]_\beta\right)^{-1}\right)=\det\left([T]_\beta\right)^{-1}=\det\left(T\right)^{-1}$$
Let $R\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$ denote an orthogonal transformation (rotation/reflection), which is to say $R^{-1}=R^\dagger$. It is expected that the absolute (hyper) volume generated by an arbitrary set of vectors is invariant under the mapping $R$ (i.e. $\omega$ is consistent). To validate this, consider the supplementary calculation:  
$$\det\left(R^{-1}\right)=\det\left(R^\dagger\right)\quad\implies\quad\det\left(R\right)^{-1}=\det\left(R\right)\quad\implies\quad\det\left(R\right)^2=1\quad\implies\quad\det\left(R\right)=\pm1$$
As expected, it instantly 
follows from the above result, and the pullback eigen equation, that the orthogonal transformation $R$ interacts with the volume-form $\omega$ accordingly:
$$R^*\omega=\det\left(R\right)\omega=\pm\,\omega\quad\implies\quad R^*\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)=\pm\,\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)\quad\text{for any}\quad v_1,\dots,v_n\in\bm V$$
It is in this sense that the volume-form consistently quantifies (hyper) volume. In this respect, regardless of the orientation, the absolute (hyper) volume of the parallelepiped generated by any orthonormal basis will be unity. This is all due to the fact that orthogonal transformations (in general) preserve the relative angles between vectors (with respect to the inner product $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$) without scaling.
