Because $\det\left(T\right)$ is intrinsic to the map $T$, it is natural to hypothesize that $\det\left(T\right)$ is a function of the spectrum (eigenvalues) of $T$. Suppose that $T$, or equivalently $[T]_\beta$, is diagonalizable. Then there exists linearly independent eigenvectors $v_1,\dots,v_n\in\bm V$ with corresponding eigenvalues $\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n\in\mathbb{R}$. According to the previously derived formula for the determinant:
\begin{align*}
\det\left(T\right)&=\frac{T^*\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)}{\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)}=\frac{\omega\left(T(v_1),\dots,T(v_n)\right)}{\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)}=\frac{\omega\left(\lambda_1v_1,\dots,\lambda_nv_n\right)}{\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)}=\frac{\lambda_1\cdots\lambda_n\,\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)}{\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)}
\\[4 mm]
&=\frac{\lambda_1\cdots\lambda_n\,\cancel{\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)}}{\cancel{\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)}}=\lambda_1\cdots\lambda_n\qquad\text{since}\qquad\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)\neq0
\end{align*}
If we establish the eigenvalues to be the principal scaling factors of the linear map pertaining to each principal direction (eigenvector), then the overall scaling factor is simply the product of each principal scale factor.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
However, in the case that $T$ is not diagonalizable over $\mathbb{R}$ or even $\mathbb{C}$ (upon extending the field), we still aim to study the spectrum of $T$. In order to do so, consider the eigenspace $E_\lambda(T)$ defined below:
$$E_{\lambda}(T)\coloneq\ker\left(T-\lambda\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}\right)\setminus\left\{0\right\}$$
Importantly, if $v\in E_\lambda(T)$, then $\left(T-\lambda\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}\right)(v)=0$ if and only if $T(v)=\lambda v$ (for $v\neq0$). Therefore, $E_\lambda(T)$ is the set of all eigenvectors with corresponding eigenvalue $\lambda$. Thus, if $\lambda$ is an eigenvalue corresponding to an eigenvector of $T$, then $E_\lambda(T)\neq\emptyset$ by definition, and thus the map $T-\lambda\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}$ cannot be injective, which implies $\det\left(T-\lambda\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}\right)=0$. We can now define the characteristic polynomial of $T$ in $\lambda$ according to:
$$p_T(\lambda)\coloneq\det\left(T-\lambda\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}\right)=\det\left([T-\lambda\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}]_\beta\right)=\det\left([T]_\beta-\lambda I\right)$$
This motivates the definition for the characteristic polynomial pertaining to any matrix $A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ as follows:
$$p_A(\lambda)\coloneq\det\left(A-\lambda I\right)\qquad\implies\qquad p_T(\lambda)=p_{[T]_\beta}(\lambda)\qquad\text{for any basis $\beta$ of $\bm V$}$$
By inspection, $p_T(\lambda)$ is in fact a polynomial in $\lambda$ with coefficients in $\mathbb{R}$, and thereby splits over $\mathbb{C}$ per the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra. Provided that $\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_m\in\mathbb{C}$ are the roots of $p_T(\lambda)$, including multiplicities, each root necessarily satisfies the condition $p_T(\lambda_i)=\det\left(T-\lambda_i\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}\right)=0$, or equivalently, $E_{\lambda_i}(T)\neq\emptyset$. In other words, $\left\{\lambda_i\right\}_{i=1}^m$ is the total collection of eigenvalues of $T$. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Furthermore, because $\deg\left(p_T(\lambda)\right)=n$, by the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra once again, it has $n$ roots counting multiplicities (i.e. $m=n$). Now, for some coefficient $\kappa\in\mathbb{C}$, it follows from the Factor Theorem:   
$$p_T(\lambda)=\kappa\prod_{i=1}^n\left(\lambda_i-\lambda\right)$$
To determine the value of $\kappa$, with the understanding that $p_T(\lambda)=\kappa\left(-1\right)^n\lambda^n+\mathcal{O}\left(\lambda^{n-1}\right)$ is continuous in $\lambda$, upon studying the polynomial asymptotically, the leading coefficient can be computed as below:
\begin{align*}
\kappa&=\lim_{\lambda\to\infty}(-\lambda)^{-n}p_T(\lambda)=\lim_{\lambda\to\infty}(-\lambda)^{-n}\det\left([T]_\beta-\lambda I\right)=\lim_{\lambda\to\infty}\det\left(-\lambda^{-1}\left([T]_\beta-\lambda I\right)\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=\lim_{\lambda\to\infty}\det\left(I-\lambda^{-1}[T]_\beta\right)=\det\left(I-\lim_{\lambda\to\infty}\lambda^{-1}[T]_\beta\right)\qquad\text{since}\quad\det\in C(\mathbb{R}^{n\times n},\mathbb{R})
\\[4 mm]
&=\det\left(I-0\right)=\det\left(I\right)=1\quad\implies\quad p_T(\lambda)=\prod_{i=1}^n\left(\lambda_i-\lambda\right)
\end{align*}
Therefore, not only do similar matrices have the same spectrum (since they share the same characteristic polynomial whose roots are the eigenvalues), but if we carefully evaluate the characteristic polynomial at zero, we arrive at the familiar result relating the determinant to the eigenvalues of the linear map:
$$\det\left(T\right)=\det\left(T-0\cdot\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}\right)=p_T(0)=\prod_{i=1}^n\left(\lambda_i-0\right)=\prod_{i=1}^n\lambda_i$$
