One neat application of the determinant is Cramer's Rule, which establishes a closed form algorithm involving the determinant for solving consistent/independent systems of linear equations. In particular, given the known parameters $A=[a_{ij}]_{i,j=1}^n\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ (full rank) and $b=[b_i]_{i=1}^n\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times1}$, and the unknown variable(s) $x=[x_j]_{j=1}^n\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times1}$, consider the linear system $Ax=b$:
\[
\begin{bmatrix}
a_{11} & \dots & a_{1n} \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
a_{n1} & \dots & a_{nn}
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
\vdots\\
x_n
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
b_1\\
\vdots\\
b_n
\end{bmatrix}
\qquad\text{(or equivalently)}\qquad
\sum_{j=1}^nx_j
\begin{bmatrix}
a_{1j}\\
\vdots\\
a_{nj}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
b_1\\
\vdots\\
b_n
\end{bmatrix}
\]
Now, construct the following matrix structure $\hat A_k^b$ by replacing the $k^\text{th}$ column of the $A$ matrix with the column $b$ as demonstrated below: 
\[
\hat A_k^b=
\begin{bmatrix}
    a_{11} & \cdots & a_{1k-1} & b_1 & a_{1k+1} & \cdots & a_{1n}\\
    \vdots & \ddots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
    a_{n1} & \cdots & a_{nk-1} & b_n & a_{nk+1} & \cdots & a_{nn}
\end{bmatrix}
\]
Upon taking the determinant of this matrix, by carefully applying the column-wise linearity and degeneracy properties of the determinant, the $k^\text{th}$ unknown (i.e. $x_k$) can be extracted from the system accordingly:  
\begin{align*}
    \det\left(\hat A_k^b\right)&=\det
    \begin{pmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{11}\\\vdots\\a_{n1}
    \end{bmatrix}
    &\cdots&
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1k-1}\\\vdots\\a_{nk-1}
    \end{bmatrix}
    &
    \begin{bmatrix}
        b_1\\\vdots\\b_n
    \end{bmatrix}
    &
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1k+1}\\\vdots\\a_{nk+1}
    \end{bmatrix}
    &\cdots&
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1n}\\\vdots\\a_{nn}
    \end{bmatrix}
\end{pmatrix}
\\[4 mm]
&=\det
\begin{pmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{11}\\\vdots\\a_{n1}
    \end{bmatrix}
    &\cdots&
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1k-1}\\\vdots\\a_{nk-1}
    \end{bmatrix}
    &
    \displaystyle\sum_{j=1}^nx_j
\begin{bmatrix}
a_{1j}\\
\vdots\\
a_{nj}
\end{bmatrix}
&
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1k+1}\\\vdots\\a_{nk+1}
    \end{bmatrix}
    &\cdots&
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1n}\\\vdots\\a_{nn}
    \end{bmatrix}
\end{pmatrix}
\\[4 mm]
\text{(linearity)}\quad&=\displaystyle\sum_{j=1}^nx_j\det
\begin{pmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{11}\\\vdots\\a_{n1}
    \end{bmatrix}
    &\cdots&
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1k-1}\\\vdots\\a_{nk-1}
    \end{bmatrix}
    &
\begin{bmatrix}
a_{1j}\\
\vdots\\
a_{nj}
\end{bmatrix}
&
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1k+1}\\\vdots\\a_{nk+1}
    \end{bmatrix}
    &\cdots&
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1n}\\\vdots\\a_{nn}
    \end{bmatrix}
\end{pmatrix}
\\[4 mm]
\text{(degeneracy)}\quad&=\displaystyle\sum_{j=1}^nx_j\delta_{jk}\det
\begin{pmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{11}\\\vdots\\a_{n1}
    \end{bmatrix}
    &\cdots&
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1k-1}\\\vdots\\a_{nk-1}
    \end{bmatrix}
    &
\begin{bmatrix}
a_{1j}\\
\vdots\\
a_{nj}
\end{bmatrix}
&
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1k+1}\\\vdots\\a_{nk+1}
    \end{bmatrix}
    &\cdots&
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1n}\\\vdots\\a_{nn}
    \end{bmatrix}
\end{pmatrix}
\\[4 mm]
&=x_k\det
\begin{pmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{11}\\\vdots\\a_{n1}
    \end{bmatrix}
    &\cdots&
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1k-1}\\\vdots\\a_{nk-1}
    \end{bmatrix}
    &
\begin{bmatrix}
a_{1k}\\
\vdots\\
a_{nk}
\end{bmatrix}
&
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1k+1}\\\vdots\\a_{nk+1}
    \end{bmatrix}
    &\cdots&
    \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1n}\\\vdots\\a_{nn}
    \end{bmatrix}
\end{pmatrix}
=x_k\det\left(A\right)
\end{align*}
To be clear, because $\mathrm{rank}\left(A\right)=n$, the columns of $A$ are linearly independent. Therefore, regarding the degeneracy property/step, the only instance when the determinant contribution is non-zero (i.e. the columns are linearly independent) is when all the columns are distinct (i.e. when $j=k$). Now, since $\det\left(A\right)\neq0$, Cramer's Rule follows directly from above:
\[
x_k=\frac{\det\left(\hat A_k^b\right)}{\det\left(A\right)}
=\det\begin{pmatrix}
    a_{11} & \cdots & a_{1k-1} & b_1 & a_{1k+1} & \cdots & a_{1n}\\
    \vdots & \ddots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
    a_{n1} & \cdots & a_{nk-1} & b_n & a_{nk+1} & \cdots & a_{nn}
\end{pmatrix}\Bigg/
\det\begin{pmatrix}
a_{11} & \dots & a_{1n} \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
a_{n1} & \dots & a_{nn}
\end{pmatrix}
\]
Although elegant, Cramer's Rule is very inefficient. Specifically, the number of operations needed to compute the determinants in the algorithm grows like $\mathcal{O}(n!)$, whereas the number of operations needed to row reduce a system via Gauss-Jordan elimination (RREF) grows like $\mathcal{O}(n^3)$.
