An immediate application of Cramer's Rule is a matrix inversion algorithm. Recall that $A^{-1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ is (contextually) the inverse of $A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ if $AA^{-1}=I$. Therefore, provided that $\mathcal{B}=\left\{\bm E_i\right\}_{i=1}^n$ is the standard ordered basis of $\mathbb{R}^{n\times1}$, one has $A\left(A^{-1}\right)_{*j}=\bm E_j$ for each $j=1,\dots,n$. This implies that the $i^\text{th}$ entry of the $j^\text{th}$ column of $A^{-1}$ can be given by Cramer's Rule as follows:  
$$\left(A^{-1}\right)_{ij}=\frac{\det\left(\hat A_i^{\bm E_j}\right)}{\det\left(A\right)}\qquad\text{which motivates the adjugate definition:}\qquad\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)\coloneq\left[\det\left(\hat A_i^{\bm E_j}\right)\right]_{i,j=1}^n$$
Using the adjugate, which exists regardless if $A$ is invertible or not, the matrix inversion algorithm can be reformatted as such without reference to indices:
$$A^{-1}=\frac{\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)}{\det\left(A\right)}\quad\text{for any full rank}\quad A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$$
We now take note of how the adjugate responds to matrix transposition. In order to do so, we need to carefully take advantage of the determinant's invariance under matrix transposition as such:
$$\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)^\top\right)_{ij}=\det\left(\hat A_j^{\bm E_i}\right)=\det\left(\left(\hat A_j^{\bm E_i}\right)^\top\right)=\det\left( \widehat{\left(A^\top\right)}_i^{\bm E_j}\right)=\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A^\top\right)\right)_{ij}\;\;\implies\;\;\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)^\top=\mathrm{adj}\left(A^\top\right)$$
Now, without assuming $\mathrm{rank}\left(A\right)=n$, because the determinant is column-wise linear, we can construct the linear functional, which takes the determinant of $A$ with one of its columns replaced by an input vector. With the understanding that $b=\sum_{j=1}^nb_j\bm E_j$, we can make the definition tangible as below:
$$\det\left(\hat A_i^{(\cdot)}\right):b\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times1}\mapsto\det\left(\hat A_i^b\right)\quad\implies\quad\det\left(\hat A_i^b\right)=\sum_{j=1}^n\det\left(\hat A_i^{\bm E_j}\right)b_j=\sum_{j=1}^n\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)\right)_{ij}b_j$$
Given some coordinate vector $b\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times1}$, if the columns of $\hat A_i^b$ are linearly dependent, then we automatically have that $\det\,(\hat A_i^b)=0$. Therefore, if on the one hand $b=A_{*k}$ for any $k\neq i$, then $\det\,(\hat A_i^b)=0$. On the other hand, if $k=i$, then $\hat A_i^b=A$, and thus $\det\,(\hat A_i^b)=\det\left(A\right)$. We can summarize the results as follows: 
$$\det\left(A\right)\delta_{ik}=\det\left(\hat A_i^{A_{*k}}\right)=\sum_{j=1}^n\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)\right)_{ij}\left(A_{*k}\right)_j=\sum_{j=1}^n\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)\right)_{ij}A_{jk}=\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)A\right)_{ik}$$
\begin{center}
    \vspace{3 mm}
    ------ which implies ------
    \vspace{1.5 mm}
\end{center}
\begin{align*}
\det\left(A\right)I=\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)A\quad&\implies\quad\det\left(A\right)I=\left(\det\left(A\right)I\right)^\top=\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)A\right)^\top=A^\top\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)^\top=A^\top\mathrm{adj}\left(A^\top\right)
\\[4 mm]
&\implies\quad\det\left(A^\top\right)I=A^\top\mathrm{adj}\left(A^\top\right)\quad\implies\quad \det\left(A\right)I=A\,\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)
\\[4 mm]
&\implies\quad \mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)A=\det\left(A\right)I=A\,\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)
\end{align*}
The most striking consequence of the above identity involving the adjugate is that it can be used to prove the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem. Keeping in mind that $\mathrm{adj}\left(A-\lambda I\right)$ is in fact a polynomial in $\lambda$ with coefficients in $\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$, by replacing each instance of $A$ with $A-\lambda I$ in the above identity:
