\\[4 mm]
&=\omega\left(\bm e_1,\dots,\bm e_{i-1},\sum_{j=1}^n\left([\mathrm{adj}\left(T\right)]_\beta\right)_{jk}\bm e_j,\bm e_{i+1},\dots,\bm e_n\right)=\sum_{j=1}^n\left([\mathrm{adj}\left(T\right)]_\beta\right)_{jk}\omega\left(\bm e_1,\dots,\bm e_{i-1},\bm e_j,\bm e_{i+1},\dots,\bm e_n\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=\sum_{j=1}^n\left([\mathrm{adj}\left(T\right)]_\beta\right)_{jk}\delta_{ij}=\left([\mathrm{adj}\left(T\right)]_\beta\right)_{ik}\quad\implies\quad [\mathrm{adj}\left(T\right)]_\beta=\mathrm{adj}\left([T]_\beta\right)
\end{align*}
Therefore, the two notions of the adjugate are representations of the same object, which is to say that they are isomorphic. Consequentially, the adjugate of $T$ satisfies the following:
$$\mathrm{adj}\left(T\right)\circ T=\det\left(T\right)\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}=T\circ\mathrm{adj}\left(T\right)\qquad\text{and}\qquad\mathrm{adj}\left(T\right)=\det\left(T\right)T^{-1}\quad\text{iff}\quad\det\left(T\right)\neq0$$
Moreover, using the intrinsic perspective of the adjugate, its properties can be further developed, which will simultaneously develop the matrix formulation. In this regard, for any vectors $v_1,\dots,v_{n-1},u\in\bm V$ and $T,U\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$, consider the treatment of map composition:
\begin{align*}
    &\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_{n-1},\mathrm{adj}\left(T\circ U\right)(u)\right)
    =\omega\left((T\circ U)(v_1),\dots,(T\circ U)(v_{n-1}),u\right)
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\omega\left(U(v_1),\dots,U(v_{n-1}),\mathrm{adj}\left(T\right)(u)\right)=\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_{n-1},\mathrm{adj}\left(U\right)\circ\mathrm{adj}\left(T\right)(u)\right)
\end{align*}
\vspace{-1.5 mm}
$$\implies\mathrm{adj}\left(T\circ U\right)=\mathrm{adj}\left(U\right)\circ\mathrm{adj}\left(T\right)\quad\text{and thus}\quad\mathrm{adj}\left(AB\right)=\mathrm{adj}\left(B\right)\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)\quad\text{for any}\quad A,B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$$
\begin{comment}
\vspace{-3 mm}\\
If it is not clear how the above matrix identity was arrived at, consider representing the above intrinsic adjugate composition identity as follows:
\begin{align*}
[\mathrm{adj}\left(T\circ U\right)]_\beta=[\mathrm{adj}\left(U\right)\circ\mathrm{adj}\left(T\right)]_\beta\quad&\implies\quad\mathrm{adj}\left([T\circ U]_\beta\right)=[\mathrm{adj}\left(U\right)]_\beta\,[\mathrm{adj}\left(T\right)]_\beta
\\[4 mm]
&\implies\quad\mathrm{adj}\left([T]_\beta[U]_\beta\right)=\mathrm{adj}\left([U]_\beta\right)\mathrm{adj}\left([T]_\beta\right)
\end{align*}
\vspace{-1.75 mm}\\
Since this holds for any such basis $\beta$, the matrices $[T]_\beta$ and $[U]_\beta$ can be replaced arbitrarily. Therefore, the adjugate is an anti-homomorphism, which is without coincidence an essential property of inversion.
\end{comment}
\newpage
Although the established algorithms for computing the determinant has had utility in developing the theory, they are not practical. Thus, we finally aim to develop a practical means of computing the determinant by exploring the notion of cofactor expansion.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
The angle of attack here is to consider expanding the determinant in a consistent manner along distinct rows/columns of the matrix. Before doing so, given a matrix $A=[a_{ik}]_{i,k=1}^n\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$, consider the $(i,k)$ sub-matrix $\hat A_{ik}$, which is obtained by deleting both the $i^\text{th}$ row and the $k^\text{th}$ column of $A$ as shown below:
\[
\hat A_{ik}=
\begin{bmatrix}
    a_{11} & \cdots & a_{1\,k-1} & a_{1\,k+1} & \cdots & a_{1n}\\
    \vdots & \ddots & \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
    a_{i-1\,1} & \cdots & a_{i-1\,k-1} & a_{i-1\,k+1} & \cdots & a_{j-1\,n}\\
    a_{i+1\,1} & \cdots & a_{i+1\,k-1} & a_{i+1\,k+1} & \cdots & a_{i+1\,n}\\
    \vdots & \ddots & \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
     a_{n1} & \cdots & a_{n\,k-1} & a_{n\,k+1} & \cdots & a_{nn}
\end{bmatrix}
\]
Be mindful not to confuse this notation with objects of the form $\hat A_k^b$ which serve a separate purpose. Now, picking up from the Leibniz determinant formula, we will begin by expanding along a fixed ($i^\text{th}$) row:  
\begin{align*}
\det\left(A\right)
&=\sum_{\sigma\in S_n}\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)\prod_{k=1}^nA_{\sigma(k)k}
=\sum_{j=1}^n\sum_{\substack{\sigma\in S_n\\ \sigma(j)=i}}
\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)\prod_{k=1}^nA_{\sigma(k)k}
=\sum_{j=1}^n
\sum_{\substack{\sigma\in S_n\\ \sigma(j)=i}}
\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)A_{ij}\prod_{k\neq j}A_{\sigma(k)k}
\\[4mm]
&=\sum_{j=1}^n
\sum_{\varrho\in S_{n-1}}
(-1)^{i+j}\mathrm{sgn}\left(\varrho\right)
A_{ij}\prod_{k=1}^{n-1}
\left(\hat A_{ij}\right)_{\varrho(k)k}
=\sum_{j=1}^n(-1)^{i+j}A_{ij}
\sum_{\varrho\in S_{n-1}}
\mathrm{sgn}\left(\varrho\right)
\prod_{k=1}^{n-1}
\left(\hat A_{ij}\right)_{\varrho(k)k}
\\[4mm]
&=\sum_{j=1}^n(-1)^{i+j}A_{ij}\det\left(\hat A_{ij}\right)
\end{align*}
If it is not clear as to why $\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)=(-1)^{i+j}\mathrm{sgn}\left(\varrho\right)$ above, because $\varrho\in S_{n-1}$ is the permutation $\sigma\in S_n$ with $j$ and $i$ removed from the domain and range respectively, the number of transpositions needed to construct $\sigma$ is $N(\sigma)\equiv N(\varrho)+(i-1)+(j-1)\equiv N(\varrho)+i+j$ (modulo 2). Therefore, we may contextually write:
$$\mathrm{sgn}\left(\sigma\right)=(-1)^{N(\sigma)}=(-1)^{N(\varrho)+
|i-j|}=(-1)^{|i-j|}(-1)^{N(\varrho)}=(-1)^{i+j}\mathrm{sgn}\left(\varrho\right)$$
This recursive algorithm for expanding the determinant is known as cofactor expansion (aka the Laplace Expansion Theorem). The term $M_{ij}\coloneq\det\,(\hat A_{ij})$ is referred to as the $(i,j)$ minor of $A$, and we call the term $C_{ij}\coloneq(-1)^{i+j}M_{ij}=(-1)^{i+j}\det\,(\hat A_{ij})$ the corresponding $(i,j)$ cofactor of $A$, which is summarized below:
$$\det\left(A\right)=\sum_{j=1}^nA_{ij}C_{ij}=\sum_{j=1}^nA_{ij}(-1)^{i+j}M_{ij}=\sum_{j=1}^n(-1)^{i+j}A_{ij}M_{ij}=\sum_{j=1}^n(-1)^{i+j}A_{ij}\det\left(\hat A_{ij}\right)$$
The critical feature of cofactor expansion is that not only could the determinant be expanded initially along any row, but because $\det\left(A\right)=\det\left(A^\top\right)$, we could also initially expand along any fixed ($j^\text{th}$) column:
$$\det\left(A\right)=\sum_{i=1}^nA_{ij}C_{ij}=\sum_{i=1}^nA_{ij}(-1)^{i+j}M_{ij}=\sum_{i=1}^n(-1)^{i+j}A_{ij}M_{ij}=\sum_{i=1}^n(-1)^{i+j}A_{ij}\det\left(\hat A_{ij}\right)$$
Moreover, upon constructing the cofactor matrix $C=[C_{ij}]_{i,j=1}^n$, it can be related directly back to the concept of the adjugate. In particular, since $A$ and $\hat A_j^{\bm E_k}$ only differ in their $j^\text{th}$ column, they share the same cofactor $C_{ij}$, and so by expanding the determinant of $\hat A_j^{\bm E_k}$ along the $j^\text{th}$ column, we obtain:
$$\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)\right)_{jk}=\det\left(\hat A_j^{\bm E_k}\right)=\sum_{i=1}^n\left(\hat A_j^{\bm E_k}\right)_{ij}C_{ij}=\sum_{i=1}^n\left(\bm E_k\right)_iC_{ij}=\sum_{i=1}^n\delta_{ik}C_{ij}=C_{kj}\quad\implies\quad\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)=C^\top$$
\newpage
One quick result that follows immediately from the cofactor expansion algorithm is that the determinant of any triangular matrix is the product of its diagonal entries. To see this, consider the matrix $A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ in the case that it is upper triangular:
$$A=
\begin{bmatrix}
a_{11} & a_{12} & \dots & a_{1n} \\
0 & a_{22} & \cdots & a_{2n} \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
0 & 0 & \cdots & a_{nn}
\end{bmatrix}
\qquad\text{noting}\qquad \hat A_{11}=
\begin{bmatrix}
a_{22} & a_{23} & \dots & a_{2n} \\
0 & a_{33} & \cdots & a_{3n} \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
0 & 0 & \cdots & a_{nn}
\end{bmatrix}
\quad\text{is also upper triangular}$$
Therefore, upon applying the cofactor expansion algorithm with respect to the first column, we obtain the following result for the evaluation of the determinant:
$$\det\left(A\right)=a_{11}\det\left(\hat A_{11}\right)-0\cdot\det\left(\hat A_{21}\right)+\cdots+(-1)^{n-1}\cdot0\cdot\det\left(\hat A_{n1}\right)=a_{11}\det\left(\hat A_{11}\right)$$
Again, due to the fact that $\hat A_{11}$ is also upper triangular, it thereby follows from $n-1$ iterations of this procedure (i.e. induction) that the determinant is given as such:
$$\det\left(A\right)=a_{11}\cdots a_{n-1\,n-1}\det\left(a_{nn}\right)=a_{11}\cdots a_{nn}$$
Keeping in mind that $A^\top$ is lower triangular, because the determinant is invariant with respect to matrix transposition, it follows that this result holds for lower triangular matrices as well:
$$\det\left(A\right)=a_{11}\cdots a_{nn}\quad\text{for any triangular matrix}\quad A=[a_{ij}]_{i,j=1}^n\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$$
As alluded to previously, this directly implies that the eigenvalues of any triangular matrix are its diagonal entries. In particular, for any triangular $A=[a_{ij}]_{i,j=1}^n\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$, because $A-\lambda I$ is triangular and its $i^\text{th}$ diagonal entry is
$(A-\lambda I)_{ii}=a_{ii}-\lambda$, one has:
$$p_A(\lambda)=\det\left(A-\lambda I\right)=\prod_{i=1}^n(a_{ii}-\lambda)\quad\implies\quad\text{the roots of $p_A(\lambda)$ are $a_{11},\dots,a_{nn}$}$$
Now, for any $A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ and $D\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}$ (not necessarily triangular), using nearly the same reasoning via inductive cofactor expansion, the determinants of the following block matrices follow as such:
$$\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} A & O_{n\times m} \\ \hline O_{m\times n} & I_m
\end{array}\right)
=\det\left(A\right)
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} I_n & O_{n\times m} \\ \hline O_{m\times n} & D \end{array}\right)=\det\left(D\right)
$$
Given another arbitrary matrix of the form $B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}$, consider the decomposition of the respective block matrix below that follows from matrix multiplication:
$$\left[\begin{array}{c|c} A & B \\ \hline O_{m\times n} & D \end{array}\right]
=
\left[\begin{array}{c|c} A & O_{n\times m} \\ \hline O_{m\times n} & I_m \end{array}\right]
\left[\begin{array}{c|c} I_n & B \\ \hline O_{m\times n} & D \end{array}\right]
$$
Using the multiplication property of the determinant, the determinant of the above block matrix can be evaluated by computing the following product of sub-determinants:
\begin{align*}
\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} A & B \\ \hline O_{m\times n} & D \end{array}\right)
&=\underbrace{\det\left(
\begin{array}{c|c} A & O_{n\times m} \\ \hline O_{m\times n} & I_m \end{array}\right)}_{=\det\left(AI_m\right)}\underbrace{\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} I_n & B \\ \hline O_{m\times n} & D \end{array}\right)}_{=\det\left(I_nD\right)}=\det\left(A\right)\det\left(D\right)
\end{align*}
Given yet another arbitrary matrix of the form $C\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$ (not necessarily a cofactor matrix), due to the symmetry of the determinant under transposition, the following determinants are necessarily equal:    
$$\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} A & B \\ \hline O_{m\times n} & D \end{array}\right)=\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} A & O_{n\times m} \\ \hline C & D \end{array}\right)=\det\left(A\right)\det\left(D\right)$$
\newpage
Building off from the last result, assume now that $A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ is invertible. In that case, consider expanding the determinant of the block matrix given as such:
\begin{align*}
    &\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} A & B \\ \hline C & D \end{array}\right)=\det\left(A\right)\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} A & B \\ \hline C & D \end{array}\right)\det\left(A\right)^{-1}=\det\left(A\right)\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} A & B \\ \hline C & D \end{array}\right)\det\left(A^{-1}\right)
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\det\left(A\right)\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} A & B \\ \hline C & D \end{array}\right)  \det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} A^{-1} & -A^{-1}B \\ \hline O_{m\times n} & I_m \end{array}\right)=\det\left(A\right)\det\left(\left[\begin{array}{c|c} A & B \\ \hline C & D \end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c|c} A^{-1} & -A^{-1}B \\ \hline O_{m\times n} & I_m \end{array}\right]\right)
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\det\left(A\right)\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} I_n & O_{n\times m} \\ \hline CA^{-1}\vphantom{\frac{;^;}{}} & D-CA^{-1}B \end{array}\right)=\det\left(A\right)\det\left(D-CA^{-1}B\right)
\end{align*}
\vspace{2 mm}
$$\implies\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} A & B \\ \hline C & D \end{array}\right)=\det\left(A\right)\det\left(D-CA^{-1}B\right)\quad\text{whenever}\quad\det\left(A\right)\neq0\phantom{\implies}$$
\vspace{1.5 mm}\\
On the other hand, if it is instead assumed that $D\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}$ is invertible, then in that case, the above determinant of the block matrix can be expanded as such:
$$\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} A & B \\ \hline C & D \end{array}\right)=\det\left(D\right)\det\left(A-BD^{-1}C\right)\quad\text{whenever}\quad\det\left(D\right)\neq0$$
As a direct consequence of the above results, consider computing the following determinant in two distinct ways as demonstrated below:
\begin{align*}
\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} I_n & B \\ \hline C & I_m \end{array}\right)&=\underbrace{\det\left(I_n\right)}_{=1}\det\left(I_m-CI_n^{-1}B\right)=\det\left(I_m-CB\right)
\\[4 mm]
\text{(alternatively)}\quad&=\underbrace{\det\left(I_m\right)}_{=1}\det\left(I_n-BI_m^{-1}C\right)=\det\left(I_n-BC\right)
\end{align*}
Therefore, upon (strictly) negating either $B$ or $C$ in the above relation, the following determinant identity (Sylvester's Determinant Theorem) holds in general within the appropriate context:
$$\det\left(I_n+BC\right)=\det\left(I_m+CB\right)$$
Yet another determinant identity arises upon demanding the commutative constraint $CD=DC$, which implies $m=n$ (i.e. $I=I_n=I_m$), whilst considering the following polynomial in $\lambda$ as shown below:
\begin{align*}
    &\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} A & B \\ \hline C & D-\lambda I\end{array}\right)=\det\left(D-\lambda I\right)\det\left(A-B(D-\lambda I)^{-1}C\right)
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\det\left(A-B(D-\lambda I)^{-1}C\right)\det\left(D-\lambda I\right)=\det\left(\left(A-B(D-\lambda I)^{-1}C\right)\left(D-\lambda I\right)\right)
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\det\left(A\left(D-\lambda I\right)-B(D-\lambda I)^{-1}C\left(D-\lambda I\right)\right)=\det\left(AD-A(\lambda I)-B(D-\lambda I)^{-1}\left(D-\lambda I\right)C\right)
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\det\left(AD-\lambda AI-BIC\right)=\det\left(AD-BC-\lambda A\right)
\end{align*}
Because this is a polynomial equation in $\lambda$, the relationship must remain algebraically consistent at $0^\text{th}$ order (i.e. when $\lambda=0$). Therefore, the following equality of determinants holds:
$$\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} A & B \\ \hline C & D\end{array}\right)=\det\left(AD-BC\right)\quad\text{whenever}\quad CD=DC$$
On the other hand, if instead the matrices $A$ and $B$ commute multiplicatively, which also implies $m=n$, then an analogous determinant formula arises as such by symmetry:
$$\det\left(\begin{array}{c|c} A & B \\ \hline C & D\end{array}\right)=\det\left(DA-CB\right)\quad\text{whenever}\quad AB=BA$$
\begin{comment}
Noting that real numbers commute multiplicatively, this a generalization of the traditional determinant law pertaining to $\mathbb{R}^{2\times 2}$, which can be derived by any prescribed determinant algorithm, and is given below:
$$\det\begin{pmatrix}
    a & b\\
    c & d
\end{pmatrix}=ad-bc\quad\text{for any}\quad a,b,c,d\in\mathbb{R}$$
\end{comment}
\newpage
We now aim to establish a connection between the determinant and the trace operation. To do so, a detour on the notion of trace will be embarked upon. In this respect, given a dual basis $\beta^*=\left\{ \theta_j\right\}_{j=1}^n$ of the dual space $\mathscr{L}(\bm V,\mathbb{R})$ with the property that $\theta_j(\bm e_i)=\delta_{ij}$, using the tensor product $\otimes$, any linear map $T\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$ can be decomposed as follows:
\begin{align*}
    T(v)&=T\left(\,\sum_{j=1}^n\left([v]_\beta\right)_j\bm e_j\right)=\sum_{j=1}^n\left([v]_\beta\right)_jT\left(\bm e_j\right)=\sum_{j=1}^n\left([v]_\beta\right)_j\sum_{i=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{ij}\bm e_i=\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{ij}\left([v]_\beta\right)_j\bm e_i
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{ij}\theta_j(x)\,\bm e_i=\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{ij}\bm e_i\otimes\theta_j\left(\cdot,v\right)\quad\text{for any}\quad v\in\bm V
    \\[4 mm]
    &\implies T=\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{ij}\bm e_i\otimes\theta_j\quad\text{for any such pairing $(\beta,\beta^*)$}
\end{align*}
Therefore, given that $\left\{u_i\right\}_{i=1}^n$ and $\left\{v_j\right\}_{j=1}^n$ are bases of $\bm V$, there exists corresponding dual vectors denoted by $\phi_i,\psi_j\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V,\mathbb{R})$ such that any such linear map $T$ can be expressed in terms of those bases over the tensor product as below:
$$T=\sum_{i=1}^nu_i\otimes\phi_i\qquad\text{and}\qquad T=\sum_{j=1}^nv_j\otimes\psi_j$$
Crucially, because $\left\{u_i\right\}_{i=1}^n$ is a basis of $\bm V$, there exists $a_{ij}\in\mathbb{R}$ such that $v_j=\sum_{i=1}^na_{ij}u_i$, and so the representation of $T$ over the basis $\left\{v_i\right\}_{i=1}^n$ can be rewritten as such:
\begin{align*}
     &\sum_{i=1}^nu_i\otimes\phi_i=T=\sum_{j=1}^n\left(\,\sum_{i=1}^na_{ij}u_i\right)\otimes\psi_j=\sum_{i=1}^nu_i\otimes\sum_{j=1}^na_{ij}\psi_j
     \\[4 mm]
     &\implies\sum_{i=1}^nu_i\otimes\left(\phi_i-\sum_{j=1}^na_{ij}\psi_j\right)=0\quad\implies\quad\phi_i-\sum_{j=1}^na_{ij}\psi_j=0\quad\implies\quad\phi_i=\sum_{j=1}^na_{ij}\psi_j
\end{align*}
With this relationship between the respective linear functionals out of the way, consider contracting the tensor product representations of $T$ as demonstrated below:
$$\sum_{j=1}^n\psi_i(v_j)=\sum_{j=1}^n\psi_j\left(\,\sum_{i=1}^na_{ij}u_i\right)=\sum_{j=1}^n\sum_{i=1}^na_{ij}\psi_i(u_i)=\sum_{i=1}^n\left(\,\sum_{j=1}^na_{ij}\psi_i\right)(u_i)=\sum_{i=1}^n\phi_i(u_i)$$
Observe that regardless of how the linear map is represented (via a basis) over the tensor product, the act of contracting the operator expansion results in an invariant quantity. This invariant is called the trace, and can thus be defined for any such basis $\beta$ as follows:
$$\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)\coloneq\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{ij}\theta_j(\bm e_i)=\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{ij}\theta_j(\bm e_i)=\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{ij}\delta_{ij}=\sum_{i=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{ii}$$
As a direct consequence of the definition, it can be shown that the trace is a linear operator in it of itself, which is to say $\mathrm{tr}\in\mathscr{L}(\mathscr{L}(\bm V),\mathbb{R})$. To verify this, for any $T,U,\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$ and $\lambda\in\mathbb{R}$, observe the identity:
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{tr}\left(T+\lambda U\right)&=\sum_{i=1}^n\left([T+\lambda U]_\beta\right)_{ii}=\sum_{i=1}^n\left(\left([T]_\beta\right)_{ii}+\lambda\left([U]_\beta\right)_{ii}\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=\sum_{i=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{ii}+\lambda\sum_{i=1}^n\left([U]_\beta\right)_{ii}=\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)+\lambda\mathrm{tr}\left(U\right)
\end{align*}
\newpage
Another significant property of the trace is that it is cyclically invariant with respect to operator composition. In particular, for any $T,U\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$, there exists $\phi_i,\psi_j\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V,\mathbb{R})$ such that $T=\sum_{i=1}^n\bm e_i\otimes\phi_i$ and $U=\sum_{j=1}^n\bm e_j\otimes\psi_j$, which implies:
\begin{align*}
    &(T\circ U)\left(v\right)=\sum_{i=1}^n\bm e_i\otimes\phi_i\left(\cdot,U(v)\right)=\sum_{i=1}^n\phi_i\left(U(v)\right)\bm e_i=\sum_{i=1}^n\phi_i\left(\,\sum_{j=1}^n\bm e_j\otimes\psi_j\left(\cdot,v\right)\right)\bm e_i
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\sum_{i=1}^n\phi_i\left(\,\sum_{j=1}^n\psi_j\left(v\right)\bm e_j\right)\bm e_i=\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n\psi_j\left(v\right)\phi_i\left(\bm e_j\right)\bm e_i=\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n\phi_i\left(\bm e_j\right)\bm e_i\otimes\psi_j\left(\cdot,v\right)\quad\text{for any}\quad v\in\bm V
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
    &\implies T\circ U=\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n\phi_i\left(\bm e_j\right)\bm e_i\otimes\psi_j\quad\overset{\text{(symmetry)}}{\iff}\quad U\circ T=\sum_{j=1}^n\sum_{i=1}^n\psi_j\left(\bm e_i\right)\bm e_j\otimes\phi_i
    \\[4 mm]
    &\implies\mathrm{tr}\left(T\circ U\right)=\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n\phi_i\left(\bm e_j\right)\psi_j\left(\bm e_i\right)=\sum_{j=1}^n\sum_{i=1}^n\psi_j\left(\bm e_i\right)\phi_i\left(\bm e_j\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left(U\circ T\right)
\end{align*}
Upon representing the linear maps $T,U$ with respect to the basis $\beta$, the above property of the trace can be re-casted in terms of matrix multiplication as follows: 
$$\mathrm{tr}\left(T\circ U\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left(U\circ T\right)\quad\implies\quad\sum_{i=1}^n\left([T]_\beta[U]_\beta\right)_{ii}=\underbrace{\sum_{i=1}^n\left([T\circ U]_\beta\right)_{ii}}_{\mathrm{tr}\left(T\circ U\right)}=\underbrace{\sum_{i=1}^n\left([U\circ T]_\beta\right)_{ii}}_{\mathrm{tr}\left(U\circ T\right)}=\sum_{i=1}^n\left([U]_\beta[T]_\beta\right)_{ii}$$
Yet another property of the trace operator is that it composes with the adjoint operator without altering its output. In particular, upon temporarily assuming $\beta$ to be orthonormal, it could thus be demonstrated:
$$\mathrm{tr}\left(T^\dagger\right)=\sum_{i=1}^n\left(\left[T^\dagger\right]_\beta\right)_{ii}=\sum_{i=1}^n\left(\left([T]_\beta\right)^\top\right)_{ii}=\sum_{i=1}^n\left([T]_\beta\right)_{ii}=\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)$$
With all of this scaffolding for the trace established, it becomes natural to extend the notion of trace from linear maps to matrices of the form $A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$, which is defined accordingly:
$$\mathrm{tr}\left(A\right)\coloneq\sum_{i=1}^nA_{ii}\qquad\text{and thus}\qquad\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left([T]_\beta\right)\quad\text{for any basis $\beta$ of $\bm V$}$$
As a result, in this context, not only is taking the trace of the transpose equivalent to only taking the trace, but the trace is a linear operator in it of itself (i.e. $\mathrm{tr}\in\mathscr{L}(\mathbb{R}^{n\times n},\mathbb{R})$). Specifically, given any $A,B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ and $\lambda\in\mathbb{R}$, this is illustrated as such:
$$\mathrm{tr}\left(A+\lambda B\right)=\sum_{i=1}^n\left(A+\lambda B\right)_{ii}=\sum_{i=1}\left(A_{ii}+\lambda B_{ii}\right)=\sum_{i=1}^nA_{ii}+\lambda\sum_{i=1}^nB_{ii}=\mathrm{tr}(A)+\lambda\mathrm{tr}\left(B\right)$$
Now, because there always exists $L_A,L_B\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$ such that $[L_A]_\beta=A$ and $[L_B]_\beta=B$, it thereby follows:
$$\mathrm{tr}\left(AB\right)=\sum_{i=1}^n\left(AB\right)_{ii}=\sum_{i=1}^n\left([L_A]_\beta[L_B]_\beta\right)_{ii}=\sum_{i=1}^n\left([L_B]_\beta[L_A]_\beta\right)_{ii}=\sum_{i=1}^n\left(BA\right)_{ii}=\mathrm{tr}\left(BA\right)$$
Furthermore, if $A$ and $B$ are similar, then there exists $Q\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ such that $A=QBQ^{-1}$, which implies similar matrices have equivalent traces as demonstrated below:
$$\mathrm{tr}\left(A\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left(QBQ^{-1}\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left(Q^{-1}QB\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left(B\right)\quad\text{whenever}\quad A\sim B$$
\newpage
Like the determinant, the trace is an intrinsic quantity associated with the linear map at hand, and so it becomes natural to hypothesize that the trace is a function of the respective spectrum of the operator. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Suppose that $T$, or equivalently $[T]_\beta$ is diagonalizable. Then there exists linearly independent eigenvectors $v_1,\dots,v_n\in\bm V$ with corresponding eigenvalues $\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n\in\mathbb{R}$ and dual vectors $\xi_1,\dots,\xi_n\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V,\mathbb{R})$ with the property $\xi_j\left(v_i\right)=\delta_{ij}$ such that:
$$T\left(v_j\right)=\lambda_jv_j=\sum_{i=1}^n\lambda_i\underbrace{\xi_i\left(v_j\right)}_{\delta_{ij}}v_i\quad\implies\quad T=\sum_{i=1}^n\lambda_iv_i\otimes\xi_i\quad\implies\quad\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)=\sum_{i=1}^n\lambda_i\underbrace{\xi_i\left(v_i\right)}_{\delta_{ii}}=\sum_{i=1}^n\lambda_i$$
It almost goes without saying, just as the trace of a linear operator is the sum of its eigenvalues, the trace of a matrix is likewise the sum of its respective eigenvalues. However, in the case that diagonalization fails over $\mathbb{R}$ or even $\mathbb{C}$ (upon extending the field), the principle can still be shown to hold. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
For any $T\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$, since $p_T(\lambda)$ splits over $\mathbb{C}$, there necessarily exists an eigenvalue $\lambda_1$ such that $p_T(\lambda_1)=0$, and thus the eigenspace $E_{\lambda_1}(T)\neq\emptyset$. Therefore, we can choose an eigenvector from $E_{\lambda_1}(T)$ and extend it to a basis $\gamma$ of $\bm V$. With the understanding that $H=[T]_{\gamma}$, for some other $H'\in\mathbb{C}^{n\times n}$, we can write:
$$
H=
\left[\begin{array}{c|c} \lambda_1 & * \\ \hline O_{n-1\,1} & H'\end{array}\right]
\quad\implies\quad p_{H}(\lambda)=\det\left(H-\lambda I_n\right)
=\left(\lambda_1-\lambda\right)\det\left(H'-\lambda I_{n-1}\right)=\left(\lambda_1-\lambda\right)p_{H'}(\lambda)
$$
Since the roots of $p_{H}(\lambda)$ are necessarily the eigenvalues of $T$, which are given by $\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n$, it therefore follows that $\lambda_2,\dots,\lambda_n$ are the roots of $p_{H'}(\lambda)$. The trace of the linear map $T$ can also be given by:
$$\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left(H\right)=\lambda_1+\mathrm{tr}\left(H'\right)$$
Because $H'$ itself is square, the preceding procedure can be applied recursively to $H'$, and so on. In particular, $\mathrm{tr}\left(H'\right)$ can be expanded just as $\mathrm{tr}\left(H\right)$ was expanded (above), and so it directly follows from induction that the trace of $T$ can be computed as such:
$$\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)=\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\lambda_j+\mathrm{tr}\left(H^{(k)}\right)\quad\&\quad\mathrm{tr}\left(H^{(n)}\right)=\lambda_n\quad\implies\quad\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)=\sum_{i=1}^n\lambda_i$$
Now that it has been proven that the trace of any linear map is always the sum of its eigenvalues, which analogously implies the trace of any square matrix is the sum of its eigenvalues, a clear connection between the determinant and trace can be established.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
However, before proceeding, the exponential map ought to be developed. Consider the smooth function $X:\mathbb{R}\to\bm V$. Keep in mind that the image of the map $t\in\mathbb{R}\mapsto[X(t)]_\beta$ can be interpreted as a smooth curve in $\mathbb{R}^n$. In this regard, provided a linear map $T\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$, let the velocity of $X$ be $T(X)$, where $X(0)=X_0\in\bm V$, which is to say the function $X$ obeys the following initial value problem:
$$\dot X=T(X)\qquad\text{such that}\qquad X(0)=X_0$$
From the theory of differential equations, there exists a unique solution to the above initial value problem, and it is given below via the formal exponential:
$$X(t)=\exp\left(tT\right)X_0\qquad\text{such that}\qquad\exp:L\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)\mapsto\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}+\sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac{L^k}{k!}$$
Alternatively, the differential equation can be casted in $\mathbb{R}^n$ by expressing everything in terms of the basis $\beta$. In particular, $[X]_\beta$ necessarily obeys the analogous initial value problem:
$$[\dot X]_\beta=[T]_\beta[X]_\beta\qquad\text{such that}\qquad[X]_\beta\left(0\right)=[X_0]_\beta$$
Just as before, the unique solution to the above initial value problem can be given via the formal exponential, which will now be defined to also accept matrix inputs as prescribed below:
$$[X]_\beta\left(t\right)=\exp\left(t[T]_\beta\right)[X_0]_\beta\qquad\text{such that}\qquad\exp:A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}\mapsto I+\sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac{A^k}{k!}$$
\newpage
Note in particular that for any such $T\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$, the exponential respects the matrix representation of $T$ over any such basis $\beta$ as demonstrated below:
$$[\exp(T)]_\beta=\left[\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}+\sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac{T^k}{k!}\right]_\beta=[\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}]_\beta+\sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac{\left[T^k\right]_\beta}{k!}=I+\sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac{\left([T]_\beta\right)^k}{k!}=\exp\left([T]_\beta\right)$$
With the additional contextual understanding that $\exp:z\in\mathbb{C}\mapsto e^z$, it is now possible to unravel the spectrum of $\exp\left(T\right)$ by considering the following augmented characteristic polynomial in $\lambda$:
\begin{align*}
p_{\exp\left(T\right)}(\exp\left(\lambda\right))&=p_{[\exp\left(T\right)]_\beta}(\exp\left(\lambda\right))=p_{\exp\left([T]_\beta\right)}(\exp\left(\lambda\right))=\det\left(\exp\left([T]_\beta\right)-\exp\left(\lambda\right)I\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=\det\left(I+\sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac{\left([T]_\beta\right)^k}{k!}-\left(1+\sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac{\lambda^k}{k!}\right)I\right)=\det\left(\,\sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac{1}{k!}\left(\left([T]_\beta\right)^k-\lambda^kI\right)\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=\det\left(\,\sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac{1}{k!}\left([T]_\beta-\lambda I\right)\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\lambda^{k-1-j}\left([T]_\beta\right)^j\right)=\det\left(\left([T]_\beta-\lambda I\right)\sum_{k=1}^\infty\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\frac{\lambda^{k-1-j}}{k!}\left([T]_\beta\right)^j\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=\underbrace{\det\left([T]_\beta-\lambda I\right)}_{p_T(\lambda)}\det\left(\,\sum_{k=1}^\infty\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\frac{\lambda^{k-1-j}}{k!}\left([T]_\beta\right)^j\right)\eqcolon p_T(\lambda)\,q_T(\lambda)
\end{align*}
Because $p_{\exp\left(T\right)}(\Lambda)$ is a degree $n$ polynomial in $\Lambda$, it has precisely $n$ complex roots counting multiplicities. Moreover, the factorization $p_{\exp\left(T\right)}(\exp\left(\lambda\right))=p_T(\lambda)\,q_T(\lambda)$ demonstrates that whenever $\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $T$, so that $p_T(\lambda)=0$, one has $p_{\exp\left(T\right)}(\exp\left(\lambda\right))=0$. Thus, if $\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n$ are the eigenvalues of $T$ counting multiplicities, then $\exp\left(\lambda_1\right),\dots,\exp\left(\lambda_n\right)$ are eigenvalues of $\exp\left(T\right)$ counting multiplicities. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Overall, this implies that the eigenvalues of $\exp\left(T\right)$ are precisely the exponentiated eigenvalues of $T$, and are given by the collection of exponentials $\left\{\exp\left(\lambda_i\right)\right\}_{i=1}^n$, which further implies Jacobi's identity:
$$\det\left(\exp\left(T\right)\right)=\prod_{i=1}^n\exp\left(\lambda_i\right)=\exp\left(\,\sum_{i=1}^n\lambda_i\right)=\exp\left(\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)\right)$$
Because the determinant and trace are basis independent, the analogous identity for the matrix representation of $T$ over the basis $\beta$ likewise follows:
$$\det\left(\exp\left([T]_\beta\right)\right)=\det\left([\exp\left(T\right)]_\beta\right)=\det\left(\exp\left(T\right)\right)=\exp\left(\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)\right)=\exp\left(\mathrm{tr}\left([T]_\beta\right)\right)$$
Since the above identity holds for any such basis $\beta$, the above matrix formula generalizes to any  matrix of the form $A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ as shown below:
$$\det\left(\exp\left(A\right)\right)=\exp\left(\mathrm{tr}\left(A\right)\right)$$
In order to achieve a superior understanding of these relationships (Jacobi's identity), it is beneficial to study the notion of divergence. To be specific, consider an $n$-dimensional parallelepiped whose vertices translate along the flow induced by $T\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$. Its (hyper) volume after deforming for a duration $t\in\mathbb{R}$ can be measured by the following (hyper) volume-form:
$$\exp\left(tT\right)^*\omega=\det\left(\exp\left(tT\right)\right)\omega=\exp\left(\mathrm{tr}\left(tT\right)\right)\omega=\exp\left(\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)t\right)\omega$$
It thereby follows that the initial rate at which the (hyper) volume of the $n$-dimensional parallelepiped changes along the flow induced by $T$ can be measured accordingly:
$$\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}\exp\left(tT\right)^*\omega=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}\exp\left(\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)t\right)\omega=\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)\left.\exp\left(\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)t\right)\right|_{t=0}\omega=\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)\omega$$
\newpage
Therefore, the (hyper) volume-form $\omega$ is an eigenvector with a corresponding eigenvalue $\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)$ of the linear operator $\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}\exp\left(tT\right)^*\in\mathscr{L}(\mathscr{L}(\bm V^n,\mathbb{R}))$. Thus, the trace of $T$ can be expressed as follows:
$$\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}\frac{\exp\left(tT\right)^*\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)}{\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)}\quad\text{for any linearly independent}\quad v_1,\dots,v_n\in\bm V$$
Alternatively, due to how the pullback interacts with the (hyper) volume-form, the trace can be expressed directly in terms of the determinant, noting $\exp\left(tT\right)^*=\left(\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}+tT\right)^*+\mathcal{O}(t^2)$, and subsequently the natural logarithm in full agreement with Jacobi's identity as demonstrated below:  
$$\mathrm{tr}\left(T\right)=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}\det\left(\exp\left(tT\right)\right)=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}\det\left(\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}+tT\right)=\ln\left(\det\left(\exp\left(T\right)\right)\right)$$
Conceptually, when the rate at which the (hyper) volume of any $n$-dimensional parallelepiped deforms (relative to the induced flow) is normalized by the (hyper) volume itself, the result only depends on the generator (i.e. the linear map $T$) of the induced flow, and not the initial shape. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
To see how this concept relates to divergence, consider the differentiable vector field $F:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^n$, and observe that the Jacobian of $F$ at the point $x=\left(x_1,\dots,x_n\right)\in\mathbb{R}^n$ is given by the following matrix:
$$J_F(x)=\left[\frac{\partial F_i}{\partial x_j}\left(x_1,\dots,x_n\right)\right]_{i,j=1}^n\qquad\text{where}\qquad F_i=\bm E_i\cdot F$$  
The divergence of $F$ at the point $x$ is (contextually) expressed according to the following formula, which depends on the Jacobian of $F$ as such:
$$\mathrm{div}\left(F\right)(x)=\sum_{i=1}^n\frac{\partial F_i}{\partial x_i}\left(x_1,\dots,x_n\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left(J_F(x)\right)$$
From this perspective, as the Jacobian of $F$ at the point $x$ is the first order linearization of $F$ at $x$, the divergence of $F$ at $x$ can be interpreted as the normalized instantaneous rate at which the (hyper) volume of an infinitesimal $n$-dimensional parallelepiped changes when deforming locally around the point $x$.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Moreover, as any matrix $A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ induces a left multiplication map $L_A:x\in\mathbb{R}^n\mapsto Ax$, which has a corresponding Jacobian
$J_{L_A}(x)=A$, it follows that $\mathrm{div}\left(L_A\right)(x)=\mathrm{tr}\left(J_{L_A}(x)\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left(A\right)$, and so the trace of any such matrix is the (global) divergence of the induced vector field (i.e. left multiplication map).
\begin{comment}
Also note that even after a change of coordinates, which transforms $J_F(x)$ into a similar matrix, because the trace is basis independent, the divergence remains invariant. This is due to the fact that divergence is fundamentally a local geometric quantity associated with the vector field. 
\end{comment}
\vspace{3 mm}\\
With Jacobi's identity now fully developed, it can be generalized. In particular, consider the differentiable matrix function $A:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ such that $\det\left(A\right)\ne0$. By carefully applying the Mean Value Theorem, the matrix  $A(t+h)$ can be locally expanded via the exponential as such: 
\begin{align*}
    A(t+h)&=A(t)\left(I+hA(t)^{-1}\frac{A(t+h)-A(t)}{h}\right)=A(t)\left(I+hA(t)^{-1}\left(\dot A(t)+\mathcal{O}(h)\right)\right)
    \\[4 mm]
    &=A(t)\left(I+hA(t)^{-1}\dot A(t)+\mathcal{O}\left(h^2\right)\right)=A(t)\exp\left(hA(t)^{-1}\dot A(t)+\mathcal{O}\left(h^2\right)\right)
\end{align*}
Now that the above local matrix expansion has been established, upon taking the determinant, Jacobi's identity can be applied, which results in the following local expansion formula of the matrix determinant:  
\begin{align*}
&\det\left(A(t+h)\right)=\det\left(A(t)\right)\det\left(\exp\left(hA(t)^{-1}\dot A(t)+\mathcal{O}\left(h^2\right)\right)\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=\det\left(A(t)\right)\exp\left(\mathrm{tr}\left(hA(t)^{-1}\dot A(t)+\mathcal{O}\left(h^2\right)\right)\right)=\det\left(A(t)\right)\exp\left(\mathrm{tr}\left(A(t)^{-1}\dot A(t)\right)h+\mathcal{O}\left(h^2\right)\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=\det\left(A(t)\right)\left(1+\mathrm{tr}\left(A(t)^{-1}\dot A(t)\right)h+\mathcal{O}\left(h^2\right)\right)=\det\left(A(t)\right)+\det\left(A(t)\right)\mathrm{tr}\left(A(t)^{-1}\dot A(t)\right)h+\mathcal{O}\left(h^2\right)
\\[4 mm]
&\implies\frac{\det\left(A(t+h)\right)-\det\left(A(t)\right)}{h}=\det\left(A(t)\right)\mathrm{tr}\left(A(t)^{-1}\dot A(t)\right)+\mathcal{O}(h)
\end{align*}
\newpage
It directly follows from taking the limit as $h$ tends to zero that the derivative of the matrix determinant can be computed as shown below via Jacobi's formula:
$$\frac{d}{dt}\det\left(A(t)\right)=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{\det\left(A(t+h)\right)-\det\left(A(t)\right)}{h}=\det\left(A(t)\right)\mathrm{tr}\left(A(t)^{-1}\dot A(t)\right)$$
We can now construct the dynamic linear map given by the differentiable function $T:t\in\mathbb{R}\mapsto T_t\in\mathscr{L}(\bm V)$, where it is imposed that $\det\left(T_t\right)\neq0$ for all $t\in\mathbb{R}$. This construction induces the differentiable function $[T]_\beta:t\in\mathbb{R}\mapsto[T_t]_\beta\in\mathbb{R}$, and thus Jacobi's formula can be extended to linear maps as such:
\begin{align*}
    \frac{d}{dt}\det\left(T_t\right)&=\frac{d}{dt}\det\left([T_t]_\beta\right)=\det\left([T_t]_\beta\right)\mathrm{tr}\left(\left([T_t]_\beta\right)^{-1}\frac{d}{dt}\,[T_t]_\beta\right)=\det\left(T_t\right)\mathrm{tr}\left(\left[T_t^{-1}\right]_\beta[\dot T_t]_\beta\right)
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\det\left(T_t\right)\mathrm{tr}\left(\left[T_t^{-1}\circ\dot T_t\right]_\beta\right)=\det\left(T_t\right)\mathrm{tr}\left(T_t^{-1}\circ\dot T_t\right)
\end{align*}
From an equivalent perspective, Jacobi's formula can be re-casted in terms of the logarithmic derivative of the determinant. In particular, it follows from the above context that one can write:
$$\frac{d}{dt}\ln\left(\det\left(A(t)\right)\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left(A(t)^{-1}\dot A(t)\right)\qquad\text{and}\qquad\frac{d}{dt}\ln\left(\det\left(T_t\right)\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left(T_t^{-1}\circ\dot T_t\right)$$
Recall that Jacobi's formula as currently established implicitly assumes the respective determinant to be non-zero. This motivates an effort to generalize Jacobi's formula using the adjugate. In particular, consider applying the total differential $d$ across the identity $\det\left(A\right)=\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)A\right)_{kk}$ with respect to the elements of the differentiable function $A:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ (i.e. $A_{ij}:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$) via the chain rule as follows:
\begin{align*}
    d\det\left(A\right)&=d\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)A\right)_{kk}=d\sum_{j=1}^n\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)\right)_{kj}A_{jk}=d\sum_{j=1}^n\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)^\top\right)_{jk}A_{jk}=d\sum_{j=1}^n\det\left(\hat A_{jk}\right)A_{jk}
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\sum_{j=1}^nd\left(\det\left(\hat A_{jk}\right)A_{jk}\right)=\sum_{j=1}^n\sum_{i,k=1}^n\frac{\partial}{\partial A_{ik}}\left(\det\left(\hat A_{jk}\right)A_{jk}\right)dA_{ik}
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\sum_{j=1}^n\sum_{i,k=1}^n\left(\frac{\partial \det\left(\hat A_{jk}\right)}{\partial A_{ik}}A_{jk}+\det\left(\hat A_{jk}\right)\frac{\partial A_{jk}}{\partial A_{ik}}\right)dA_{ik}=\sum_{j=1}^n\sum_{i,k=1}^n\left(0\cdot A_{jk}+\det\left(\hat A_{jk}\right)\delta_{ij}\right)dA_{ik}
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\sum_{j=1}^n\sum_{i,k=1}^n\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)^\top\right)_{jk}\delta_{ij}dA_{ik}=\sum_{j=1}^n\sum_{k=1}^n\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)^\top\right)_{jk}dA_{jk}=\sum_{k=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)\right)_{kj}dA_{jk}
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\sum_{k=1}^n\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)dA\right)_{kk}=\mathrm{tr}\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A\right)dA\right)\quad\implies\quad\frac{d}{dt}\det\left(A(t)\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(A(t)\right)\dot A(t)\right)
\end{align*}
Just as before, this generalization of Jacobi's formula can be (contextually) extended to linear maps via an analogous process as demonstrated below:
\begin{align*}
    \frac{d}{dt}\det\left(T_t\right)&=\frac{d}{dt}\det\left([T_t]_\beta\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left(\mathrm{adj}\left([T_t]_\beta\right)\frac{d}{dt}\,[T_t]_\beta\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left([\mathrm{adj}\left(T_t\right)]_\beta[\dot T_t]_\beta\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left([\mathrm{adj}\left(T_t\right)\circ\dot T_t]_\beta\right)
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\mathrm{tr}\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(T_t\right)\circ\dot T_t\right)\qquad\text{or via differentials}\qquad d\det\left(T\right)=\mathrm{tr}\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(T\right)\circ dT\right)
\end{align*}
As a side note, now that differentiation of the determinant is an accessible calculation, the coefficients of the characteristic polynomial can be determined directly using Taylor's theorem:
$$p_T(\lambda)=\sum_{k=0}^n\frac{\lambda^k}{k!}\left.\frac{d^k}{d\lambda^k}\right|_{\lambda=0}\det\left(T-\lambda\mathrm{id}_{\bm V}\right)=\det\left(T\right)+\left(-1\right)^{n-1}\mathrm{tr}\left(\mathrm{adj}\left(T\right)\right)\lambda+\cdots+\left(-1\right)^n\lambda^n$$
\newpage
At this point, the determinant has been fully developed within the scope of linear algebra. With that said, within the realm of calculus and differential geometry, there is much to be explored. Prerequisites moving forward involve vector calculus with respect to manifolds.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
An immediate application of the established scaffolding is the ability to generalize the notion of (hyper) volume to curvilinear shapes. In particular, given an $n$-dimensional Riemannian manifold $(M,g)$, with the understanding that $\beta=\left\{\bm e_i\right\}_{i=1}^n$ is a coordinate frame of $TM$, the metric tensor is a symmetric bilinear-form defined by the inner product as given by $g=\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$, where $g_{ij}=\langle\bm e_i,\bm e_j\rangle$.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
For convenience, given that $\bm\pi:T_pM\to\{p\}$, let $u,v\in T_*M$ mean $\bm\pi(u)=\bm\pi(v)$ by definition. Noting that the metric tensor induces an $n\times n$ matrix whose $ij$ component is $g_{ij}$, we can subsequently define the Gram matrix denoted by $G:(TM)^n\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ as specified below:
$$G:v_1,\dots,v_n\in T_*M\mapsto[\langle v_i,v_j\rangle]_{i,j=1}^n\quad\implies\quad G_{ij}\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)=\langle v_i,v_j\rangle=\sum_{k=1}^n\sum_{\ell=1}^n g_{k\ell}\left([v_i]_\beta\right)_k\left([v_j]_\beta\right)_\ell$$
Crucially, because the metric tensor is positive definite (i.e. $\langle v,v\rangle>0$ iff $v\neq0$), it follows that the image of $G$ must be as well. To be specific, for any $v_1,\dots,v_n\in T_*M$ and $x=\sum_{i=1}^nx_i\bm E_i\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times1}$, one always has: 
$$x^\top G\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)x=\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^nx_iG_{ij}\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)x_j=\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^nx_i\left\langle v_i,v_j\right\rangle x_j=\left\langle\sum_{i=1}^nx_iv_i,\sum_{j=1}^nx_jv_j\right\rangle\geq0$$
Consequently, the eigenvalues of $G$ are non-negative, and so $\det\left(G\right)\geq0$ since the determinant is the product of the eigenvalues. An important note to make is that for any given point $p\in M$, a basis of $T_pM$, denoted by $\alpha=\left\{\hat{\bm n}_i\right\}_{i=1}^n$, can always be chosen such that $\langle\hat{\bm n}_i,\hat{\bm n}_j\rangle=\delta_{ij}$ (i.e. $\alpha$ is orthonormal).  
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Now, consider the $n$-dimensional parallelepiped generated by the tangent vectors $u_1,\dots,u_n\in T_pM$ such that $u_i=U\left(\hat{\bm n}_i\right)$ for some $U\in\mathscr{L}(T_pM)$. The corresponding signed (hyper) volume at the point $p$ is thus given by $\omega\left(u_1,\dots,u_n\right)=U^*\omega\left(\hat{\bm n}_1,\dots,\hat{\bm n}_n\right)=\det\left(U\right)$, and so with that in mind, observe the following:
\begin{align*}
\langle u_i,u_j\rangle&=\langle U\left(\hat{\bm n}_i\right),U\left(\hat{\bm n}_j\right)\rangle=\left\langle\hat{\bm n}_i,\left(U^\dagger\circ U\right)\left(\hat{\bm n}_j\right)\right\rangle=\left\langle\hat{\bm n}_i,\sum_{k=1}^n\left(\left[U^\dagger\circ U\right]_{\alpha}\right)_{kj}\hat{\bm n}_k\right\rangle
\\[4 mm]
&=\sum_{k=1}^n\left(\left[U^\dagger\circ U\right]_{\alpha}\right)_{kj}\langle\hat{\bm n}_i,\hat{\bm n}_k\rangle=\sum_{k=1}^n\left(\left[U^\dagger\circ U\right]_{\alpha}\right)_{kj}\delta_{ik}=\left(\left[U^\dagger\circ U\right]_{\alpha}\right)_{ij}
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
&\implies G\left(u_1,\dots,u_n\right)=[\langle u_i,u_j\rangle]_{i,j=1}^n=\left[U^\dagger\circ U\right]_{\alpha}
\\[4 mm]
&\implies \det\left(G\left(u_1,\dots,u_n\right)\right)=\det\left(\left[U^\dagger\circ U\right]_{\alpha}\right)=\det\left(U^\dagger\circ U\right)=\det\left(U^\dagger\right)\det\left(U\right)=\det\left(U\right)^2
\\[4 mm]
&\implies\det\left(U\right)=\pm\sqrt{\det\left(G\left(u_1,\dots,u_n\right)\right)}\quad\implies\quad\left|\omega\left(u_1,\dots,u_n\right)\right|=\sqrt{\det\left(G\left(u_1,\dots,u_n\right)\right)}
\end{align*}
In this light, because the above identity holds for any such tangent vectors $u_1,\dots,u_n\in T_pM$, the respective relation could be re-casted in terms of the unsigned (hyper) volume functional defined below:
$$\mathrm{Vol}:v_1,\dots,v_n\in T_*M\mapsto\left|\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)\right|\quad\implies\quad\mathrm{Vol}=\sqrt{\det\left(G\right)}$$
With this new definition at hand, for any $T\in\mathscr{L}(T_*M)$, it follows that the unsigned (hyper) volume functional composes with any such linear map $T$ as demonstrated here:
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{Vol}\left(T(v_1),\dots,T(v_n)\right)&=\left|\omega\left(T(v_1),\dots,T(v_n)\right)\right|=\left|T^*\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)\right|=\left|\det\left(T\right)\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)\right|
\\[4 mm]
&=\left|\det\left(T\right)\right|\left|\omega\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)\right|=\left|\det\left(T\right)\right|\mathrm{Vol}\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)\quad\text{for any}\quad v_1,\dots,v_n\in T_*M
\end{align*}
As an alternative result, for any such tangent vectors $v_1,\dots,v_n\in T_*M$, the determinant of the Gram matrix must transform accordingly in order to preserve the scaling feature derived just previously:
\begin{align*}
&\sqrt{\det\left(G\left(T(v_1),\dots,T(v_n)\right)\right)}=\mathrm{Vol}\left(T(v_1),\dots,T(v_n)\right)=\left|\det\left(T\right)\right|\mathrm{Vol}\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=\left|\det\left(T\right)\right|\sqrt{\det\left(G\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)\right)}\quad\implies\quad\det\left(G\left(T(v_1),\dots,T(v_n)\right)\right)=\det\left(T\right)^2\det\left(G\left(v_1,\dots,v_n\right)\right)
\end{align*}
Now, let $\Omega\subset M$ be a connected (hyper) volumetric region of the manifold equipped with an atlas given by $\left\{(\mathcal{U}_i,\varphi_i)\right\}_{i=1}^N$, where $\varphi_i:\Omega_i\subseteq\Omega\to\mathcal{U}_i\subset\mathbb{R}^n$. Furthermore, each coordinate domain $\mathcal{U}_i$ can be further broken down into smaller constituents $\mathcal{U}_{ij}$. To summarize, $\Omega$ and each $\mathcal{U}_i$ can be decomposed as follows:
$$\Omega=\bigcup_{i=1}^N\varphi^{-1}_i\left(\mathcal{U}_i\right)\qquad\text{and}\qquad\mathcal{U}_i=\bigcup_{j=1}^{m_i}\mathcal{U}_{ij}$$
Via the pushforward $d\varphi^{-1}_i\in\mathscr{L}(\mathbb{R}^n,T\Omega_i)$, given any reference point $p\in\Omega_i$, vectors in $\mathbb{R}^n$ (i.e. $T_{\varphi(p)}\mathbb{R}^n$) can be mapped to the tangent space $T_p\Omega_i$. Upon declaring that $\mathcal{B}=\left\{\bm E_i\right\}_{i=1}^n$ is the standard ordered basis of $\mathbb{R}^n$, we define the coordinate frame $\beta_i=\{\bm e^i_k\}_{k=1}^n$ of $T\Omega_i$ by $(d\varphi^{-1}_i)_{\varphi_i(p)}:\bm E_k\mapsto \bm e^i_k(p)$, for any $p\in\Omega_i$. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
With this at hand, note that there exists $\Delta x_{ijk}\geq0$ such that $\mathrm{proj}_{\bm E_k}(\mathcal{U}_{ij})=\Delta x_{ijk}\bm E_k$. In other words, the extent of each sub-coordinate domain $\mathcal{U}_{ij}$ can be projected along each respective coordinate axis. From an equivalent perspective, using the standard inner product of $\mathbb{R}^n$ (i.e. the dot product), we can write these domain extents as $\Delta x_{ijk}=\bm E_k\cdot\mathrm{proj}_{\bm E_k}(\mathcal{U}_{ij})$. Therefore, by the Mean Value Theorem, the (hyper) volume of $\mathcal{U}_{ij}$ with respect to the dot product is defined and bounded accordingly:
$$\mathrm{Vol}\left(\mathcal{U}_{ij}\right)\coloneq\int_{\mathcal{U}_{ij}}d^nx=\Delta x_{ij1}\cdots\Delta x_{ijn}+o\left(\Delta x_{ij1}\cdots\Delta x_{ijn}\right)$$
The vectors that span this (hyper) volume upper limit are of course $\Delta x_{ij1}\bm E_1,\dots,\Delta x_{ijn}\bm E_n$. In this regard, using the metric tensor in conjunction with the pushforward, given a reference point in the coordinate chart $\bar x_{ij}=\mathrm{mean}(\mathcal{U}_{ij})$, the corresponding volume of $\varphi_i^{-1}(\mathcal{U}_{ij})$ is defined and bounded as below:
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{Vol}\left(\varphi_i^{-1}\left(\mathcal{U}_{ij}\right)\right)&\coloneq\int_{\mathcal{U}_{ij}}\mathrm{Vol}\left(d\varphi^{-1}_i\left(dx_1\bm E_1\right),\dots,d\varphi^{-1}_i\left(dx_n\bm E_n\right)\right)\coloneq\int_{\mathcal{U}_{ij}}\mathrm{Vol}\left(d\varphi^{-1}_i\left(\bm E_1\right),\dots,d\varphi^{-1}_i\left(\bm E_n\right)\right)d^nx
\\[4 mm]
&=\mathrm{Vol}\left(\left(d\varphi^{-1}_i\right)_{\bar x_{ij}}\left(\bm E_1\right),\dots,\left(d\varphi^{-1}_i\right)_{\bar x_{ij}}\left(\bm E_n\right)\right)\mathrm{Vol}\left(\mathcal{U}_{ij}\right)+o\left(\mathrm{Vol}\left(\mathcal{U}_{ij}\right)\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=\mathrm{Vol}\left(\bm e^i_1\circ\varphi^{-1}\left(\bar x_{ij}\right),\dots,\bm e^i_n\circ\varphi^{-1}\left(\bar x_{ij}\right)\right)\mathrm{Vol}\left(\mathcal{U}_{ij}\right)+o\left(\mathrm{Vol}\left(\mathcal{U}_{ij}\right)\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=\sqrt{\det\left(G\left(\bm e^i_1,\dots,\bm e^i_n\right)\right)\circ\varphi^{-1}\left(\bar x_{ij}\right)}\,\Delta x_{ij1}\cdots\Delta x_{ijn}+o\left(\Delta x_{ij1}\cdots\Delta x_{ijn}\right)
\end{align*}
At a heuristic level, by partitioning $\mathcal{U}_i$ into smaller pieces without bound (i.e. $m_i\to\infty$), because $\Delta x_{ijk}$ tends to zero, so do the higher order terms $o(\Delta x_{ij1}\cdots\Delta x_{ijn})$, and thus, if the limit exists, with the understanding that $\varphi_i^*\det(g)\coloneq\det(G(\bm e^i_1,\dots,\bm e^i_n))$, the (hyper) volume of $\varphi^{-1}_i(\mathcal{U}_i)$ is given as such:
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{Vol}\left(\varphi^{-1}_i\left(\mathcal{U}_i\right)\right)&=\lim_{m_i\to\infty}\sum_{j=1}^{m_i}\mathrm{Vol}\left(\varphi^{-1}_i\left(\mathcal{U}_{ij}\right)\right)=\lim_{m_i\to\infty}\sum_{j=1}^{m_i}\sqrt{\det\left(G\left(\bm e^i_1,\dots,\bm e^i_n\right)\right)\circ\varphi^{-1}_i\left(\bar x_{ij}\right)}\,\Delta x_{ij1}\cdots\Delta x_{ijn}
\\[4 mm]
&=\int_{\mathcal{U}_i}\sqrt{\det\left(G\left(\bm e^i_1,\dots,\bm e^i_n\right)\right)\circ\varphi^{-1}_i(x)}\,d^nx=\int_{\mathcal{U}_i}\sqrt{\varphi_i^*\det\left(g\right)\circ\varphi^{-1}_i(x)
}\,d^nx
\end{align*}
Lastly, the total (hyper) volume of the (hyper) volumetric region $\Omega$ is thereby the sum of the partitioned contributions as demonstrated below:
$$\mathrm{Vol}\left(\Omega\right)=\sum_{i=1}^N\mathrm{Vol}\left(\varphi^{-1}_i\left(\mathcal{U}_i\right)\right)=\sum_{i=1}^N\int_{\mathcal{U}_i}\sqrt{\varphi_i^*\det\left(g\right)\circ\varphi^{-1}_i(x)
}\,d^nx$$
\newpage
Now, upon forgoing the previous context, let $\mathcal D,\mathcal U\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ be coordinate domains related by the transition map diffeomorphism $F:\mathcal U\to\mathcal D$. Tile $\mathbb{R}^n$ by a uniform grid of coordinate boxes of volume $\mathrm{Vol}(\mathcal U)/N$ with side lengths $\Delta u$, and let $\mathcal U_1,\dots,\mathcal U_N$ be those boxes lying entirely within $\mathcal U$, so that each is a genuine box:
$$
\mathrm{Vol}\left(\mathcal U_i\right)=\mathrm{Vol}\left(\mathcal U\right)/N\qquad\text{and}\qquad\mathrm{proj}_{\bm E_j}\left(\mathcal U_i\right)=\Delta u\bm E_j\qquad\text{where}\qquad\Delta u=\sqrt[n]{\mathrm{Vol}\left(\mathcal U\right)/N}
$$
Upon noting that $||u-\bar u_i||=\mathcal{O}(\Delta u)=\mathcal{O}(N^{-1/n})$ for any given $u,\bar u_i\in\mathcal{U}_i$, the image of $\mathcal U_i$ under $F$ can be expanded (set-wise) to first order via Taylor's Theorem with an appropriate remainder:
$$
F(\mathcal U_i)=F(\bar u_i)+J_F\left(\bar u_i\right)\left(\mathcal U_i-\bar u_i\right)+r(\mathcal U_i,\bar u_i)
\qquad\text{where}\qquad
\|r(\mathcal U_i,\bar u_i)\|=\mathcal O\left(\max_{u\in\mathcal U_i}\|u-\bar u_i\|^2\right)=\mathcal O\left(N^{-2/n}\right)
$$
Since $\|r(\mathcal U_i,\bar u_i)\|=\mathcal O\left(N^{-2/n}\right)$, the translated region $F(\mathcal U_i)-F(\bar u_i)$ is contained in $J_F(\bar u_i)$ applied to the box with each side expanded from $\Delta u$ to $\Delta u+|\mathcal O(N^{-2/n})|$, which is denoted by $\mathcal{U}_i\oplus\mathcal{O}(N^{-2/n})$. Analogously, the respective box with its sides shrunk from $\Delta u$ to $\Delta u-|\mathcal{O}(N^{-2/n})|$ is denoted by $\mathcal{U}_i\ominus\mathcal{O}(N^{-2/n})$.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
In the opposite direction, since $F$ is a diffeomorphism, by the chain rule, $J_{F^{-1}}(F(\bar u_i))=J_F(\bar u_i)^{-1}$, which implies, for any $x\in F(\bar u_i)+J_F(\bar u_i)\big(\mathcal U_i\oplus\mathcal{O}(N^{-2/n})\big)$, Taylor's Theorem applied to $F^{-1}$ at $F(\bar u_i)$ gives:
$$
F^{-1}(x)=\bar u_i+J_F(\bar u_i)^{-1}\big(x-F(\bar u_i)\big)+s(x,\bar u_i)\qquad\text{where}\qquad\|s(x,\bar u_i)\|=\mathcal O\left(N^{-2/n}\right)
$$
Since $J_F(\bar u_i)^{-1}$ induces a fixed linear (left multiplication) map, for any $x\in F(\bar u_i)+J_F(\bar u_i)(\mathcal U_i\ominus\mathcal{O}(N^{-2/n}))$, the point $\bar u_i+J_F(\bar u_i)^{-1}(x-F(\bar u_i))$ sits at least $\mathcal O(N^{-2/n})$ inside $\mathcal U_i$, and so the perturbation $s(x,\bar u_i)$ cannot push $F^{-1}(x)$ outside $\mathcal U_i$, which implies $x\in F(\mathcal U_i)$. Therefore, the region $F(\mathcal U_i)$ contains $J_F(\bar u_i)$ applied to $\mathcal U_i\ominus\mathcal{O}(N^{-2/n})$. Overall, the volume generated by $F(\mathcal{U}_i)$ can be bounded as such:
\begin{align*}
&\mathrm{Vol}\left(F(\mathcal U_i)\right)=\mathrm{Vol}\left(J_F(\bar u_i)\left(\left(\Delta u\pm\left|\mathcal{O}\left(N^{-2/n}\right)\right|\right)\bm E_1\right),\dots,J_F(\bar u_i)\left(\left(\Delta u\pm\left|\mathcal{O}\left(N^{-2/n}\right)\right|\right)\bm E_n\right)\right)
\\[4 mm]
&=\left|\det\left(J_F(\bar u_i)\right)\right|\left(\Delta u\pm\left|\mathcal{O}\left(N^{-2/n}\right)\right|\right)^n\underbrace{\mathrm{Vol}\left(\bm E_1,\dots,\bm E_n\right)}_{=1}=\left|\det\left(J_F(\bar u_i)\right)\right|\left(\sqrt[n]{\mathrm{Vol}\left(\mathcal U\right)/N}+\mathcal{O}\left(N^{-2/n}\right)\right)^n
\\[4mm]
&=\left|\det\left(J_F(\bar u_i)\right)\right|\left(\mathrm{Vol}(\mathcal U)/N+\mathcal O\left(N^{-(n+1)/n}\right)\right)=\left|\det\left(J_F\left(\bar u_i\right)\right)\right|\mathrm{Vol}\left(\mathcal U_i\right)+\mathcal O\left(N^{-(n+1)/n}\right)
\end{align*}
From there, given any integrable function $f:\mathcal D\to\mathbb R$, using the transition map $F$ in conjunction with the Jacobian determinant $\det\left(J_F\right)$, the function $f$ can be integrated as follows:
\begin{align*}
    \int_\mathcal D f\left(x\right)d^nx&=\lim_{N\to\infty}\sum_{i=1}^Nf\left(F(\bar u_i)\right)\mathrm{Vol}\left(F(\mathcal U_i)\right)
    \\[4mm]
    &=\lim_{N\to\infty}\sum_{i=1}^Nf\left(F(\bar u_i)\right)\left(\left|\det\left(J_F\left(\bar u_i\right)\right)\right|\mathrm{Vol}\left(\mathcal U_i\right)+\mathcal O\left(N^{-(n+1)/n}\right)\right)
    \\[4mm]
    &=\lim_{N\to\infty}\sum_{i=1}^Nf\left(F(\bar u_i)\right)\left|\det\left(J_F\left(\bar u_i\right)\right)\right|\mathrm{Vol}\left(\mathcal U_i\right)+\lim_{N\to\infty}\sum_{i=1}^N\mathcal O\left(N^{-(n+1)/n}\right)
    \\[4mm]
    &=\int_\mathcal U f\left(F(u)\right)\left|\det\left(J_F\left(u\right)\right)\right|d^nu+\cancel{\lim_{N\to\infty}\mathcal O\left(N^{-1/n}\right)}
\end{align*}
Keeping in mind that $\mathcal U=F^{-1}(\mathcal D)$, via the pullback $F^*:f\mapsto f\circ F$, the following change of variables procedure can thereby be executed as a means of computing the above $n$-dimensional integral:
$$
\boxed{\;\int_{\mathcal D}f\left(x\right)d^nx=\int_{F^{-1}(\mathcal D)}F^*f\left(u\right)\left|\det\left(J_F\left(u\right)\right)\right|d^nu\;}
$$
