One key insight to make is that the choice of coordinates to describe the intrinsic (hyper) volumetric region $\Omega$ is completely arbitrary. In this respect, whilst simplifying matters, suppose $\{(\mathcal{U},\varphi)\}$ and $\{(\mathcal R,\psi)\}$ are both atlases of $\Omega$ with corresponding frames $\beta=\left\{\bm e_i\right\}_{i=1}^n$ and $\gamma=\left\{\bm\varepsilon_i\right\}_{i=1}^n$ of the tangent bundle $T\Omega$. The (hyper) volume of the (hyper) volumetric region $\Omega$ can thereby be expressed in terms of both charts:
$$\mathrm{Vol}\left(\Omega\right)=\int_{\mathcal{U}}\sqrt{\det\nolimits_\varphi(g)}\,d^nx=\int_{\mathcal R}\sqrt{\det\nolimits_\psi(g)}\,d^ny$$
In order to further relate these two integral representations of $\mathrm{Vol}\left(\Omega\right)$, first take note of the transition map $\psi\circ\varphi^{-1}:\mathcal{U}\to\mathcal R$, which relates the coordinate domains $\mathcal{U}$ and $\mathcal R$ with an invertible map. Using the pushforward, for any $p\in\Omega$, the basis vectors of $T_p\Omega$ can be defined accordingly:
$$(d\varphi^{-1})_{\varphi(p)}:\bm E_i\mapsto\bm e_i(p)\qquad\text{and}\qquad(d\psi^{-1})_{\psi(p)}:\bm E_i\mapsto\bm\varepsilon_i(p)$$
Contextually, noting that $(d\psi)^{-1}=d\psi^{-1}$, observe how cyclic composition of these pushforwards produces basis transition maps over $T_*\Omega$ and $\mathbb{R}^n$. For example,  $d\left(\varphi^{-1}\circ\psi\right)=d\varphi^{-1}d\psi$ and $d\left(\psi\circ\varphi^{-1}\right)=d\psi\,d\varphi^{-1}$. Thus, the following Jacobian (matrix) representations can be constructed:
$$J_\varphi^\psi(p)=\left[d\left(\psi\circ\varphi^{-1}\right)_{\varphi(p)}\right]_\mathcal{B}\qquad\text{and}\qquad J^\varphi_\psi(p)=\left[d\left(\varphi\circ\psi^{-1}\right)_{\varphi(p)}\right]_\mathcal{B}\qquad\text{noting}\qquad\left(J_\varphi^\psi(p)\right)^{-1}=J_\psi^\varphi(p)$$
Due to the cyclic operator determinant property, the Jacobian determinant can thereby be expressed as such: 
\begin{align*}
    \det\left(J_\varphi^\psi(p)\right)&=\det\left(\left[d(\psi\circ\varphi^{-1})_{\varphi(p)}\right]_\mathcal{B}\right)=\det\left(d(\psi\circ\varphi^{-1})_{\varphi(p)}\right)=\det((d\psi)_p\left(d\varphi^{-1})_{\varphi(p)}\right)
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\det\left((d\varphi^{-1})_{\varphi(p)}(d\psi)_p\right)=\det\left(d(\varphi^{-1}\circ\psi)_p\right)
